WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:20.000
Downloaded from MoviesMod.at

01:11.717 --> 01:14.804
Watch your step. Thank
you. Watch your step.

01:22.688 --> 01:25.315
Morning.
Morning. Oh, excuse me.

01:25.439 --> 01:27.860
All set for the week?
Going to be busy.

01:27.985 --> 01:30.194
Don't I know it.

01:48.672 --> 01:51.925
Hi, Patty. Hi, Fay.
Hi.

02:31.339 --> 02:32.966
Still thinking
of going to London?

02:33.091 --> 02:35.886
I don't know. I'd like to,
Reg isn't keen.

02:43.476 --> 02:46.187
Good weekend?
All right.

02:46.312 --> 02:47.438
Frank take you out?

02:47.563 --> 02:48.856
Took himself out.

02:48.982 --> 02:50.775
He went fishing.
Hm.

02:52.485 --> 02:54.571
This is where
I am going to start you off.

02:54.695 --> 02:57.490
Strewth.
Look at that.

02:57.615 --> 03:00.660
I think I might leave you with
these ladies. You follow me.

03:00.785 --> 03:03.538
I hope she's better than the
one we had last Christmas.

03:03.663 --> 03:06.124
Morning, Miss Cartwright.

03:06.250 --> 03:08.794
Miss Baines, Mrs. Williams,
this is Miss Miles.

03:08.918 --> 03:11.294
Um...
L-Lisa.

03:11.421 --> 03:13.006
Lisa Miles.

03:13.131 --> 03:14.382
She'll be helping you out

03:14.506 --> 03:16.635
with the Christmas rush
and the sales.

03:16.759 --> 03:18.302
Right, there we are.

03:18.427 --> 03:21.223
These ladies will
show you the ropes.

03:21.346 --> 03:22.723
Right, then.

03:22.849 --> 03:25.060
I hope you like hard work.

03:39.907 --> 03:42.534
It's very nice to see you
here. Have a lovely day.

03:44.246 --> 03:46.623
Have you...?
Have you got a list?

03:56.633 --> 03:58.717
Oh! I'm sorry.
Excuse me.

03:58.843 --> 04:01.304
I was really hoping you
could adjust the darts.

04:01.428 --> 04:03.639
I'm afraid we can only
do hems at the moment.

04:03.765 --> 04:05.224
Oh, in the racks over there.

04:05.349 --> 04:07.310
Make sure you arrange them
according to size.

04:07.435 --> 04:08.477
Okay.

04:08.602 --> 04:11.814
Would you have this in an SW?

04:11.940 --> 04:15.485
Oh, I'm not sure.
It's my first day.

04:15.610 --> 04:17.611
Bloody staff don't know
anything these days.

04:17.736 --> 04:19.156
Don't know where they find them.

04:22.908 --> 04:24.619
Oh, I'm sorry.

04:24.743 --> 04:25.996
Don't worry, madam.

04:27.331 --> 04:28.413
Uh, Lisa.

04:28.540 --> 04:30.167
Miss Miles.

04:30.291 --> 04:31.709
Bucket and mop, please, dear.

04:33.002 --> 04:34.002
In the storeroom.

04:36.673 --> 04:37.882
Where'd you meet him?

04:38.008 --> 04:39.717
He works with Myra's boyfriend.

04:39.842 --> 04:41.636
Reckoned he was irresistible.

04:41.760 --> 04:43.346
Have I got news for him.

04:43.471 --> 04:44.890
He was all over me.

04:45.014 --> 04:46.891
They only want one thing.

04:47.016 --> 04:48.851
All of them.

04:48.976 --> 04:50.895
Maybe not all of them.

04:52.022 --> 04:53.022
What do you mean?

04:53.105 --> 04:55.608
Nothing.

04:55.733 --> 04:57.651
Frank's just a bit...

04:59.863 --> 05:01.114
Inattentive?

05:07.119 --> 05:08.453
Oh, really.

05:09.997 --> 05:12.125
Just left school,
have you, Lisa?

05:12.251 --> 05:13.584
Done the Inter, have you?

05:13.709 --> 05:15.253
No. The Leaving.

05:15.379 --> 05:17.964
The Leaving?
How old are you?

05:18.090 --> 05:20.091
I'm 16.

05:20.216 --> 05:22.385
I'll be 17 in February.

05:22.509 --> 05:24.762
I'd been working
in the shoe factory

05:24.888 --> 05:27.056
for two years when I was 16.

05:27.182 --> 05:28.599
Are you gonna be a teacher?

05:28.725 --> 05:29.809
Oh, no.

05:29.934 --> 05:31.644
What, then?

05:32.938 --> 05:34.813
I'm going to be a poet.

05:34.940 --> 05:36.774
A poet?

05:36.900 --> 05:39.651
I don't reckon there would
be much money in that.

05:39.778 --> 05:43.489
Um, well, I mean,
I'd like to try to be a poet.

05:43.614 --> 05:44.907
Or an actress.

05:45.033 --> 05:46.410
An actress?

05:46.533 --> 05:49.078
Like at the Tivoli?
In the chorus line?

05:49.204 --> 05:52.832
Well, no... No, I meant more
real theater, you know?

05:52.957 --> 05:56.711
Uh, Shakespeare,
Eugene O'Neill, Molière.

05:56.836 --> 05:58.545
Mollie-who?

06:00.757 --> 06:03.092
Hmm. Never heard of her.

06:05.136 --> 06:06.470
Mind your step.

06:08.306 --> 06:09.473
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

06:10.557 --> 06:11.976
Hey.

06:16.479 --> 06:18.650
Good afternoon, ladies.

06:18.774 --> 06:20.860
Beautiful day, is it not?

06:20.985 --> 06:23.780
How lucky we all are
to live in such a place.

06:23.904 --> 06:26.074
Ah.

06:26.198 --> 06:28.327
You must be the new<i> jeune fille.</i>

06:29.286 --> 06:31.078
Soon, you will come to me.

06:31.204 --> 06:34.498
Some help I can use
during this season.

06:37.210 --> 06:38.294
Who's that?

06:38.420 --> 06:41.047
Magda, one of them reffos.

06:41.172 --> 06:43.383
Refugees. Migrants.

06:43.506 --> 06:45.260
Comes out here
and lords it over us

06:45.384 --> 06:46.677
with some cock-and-bull story

06:46.802 --> 06:50.556
about a fashion job
in Paris before the war.

06:50.681 --> 06:52.809
Tell you what, she looks all right though.

06:52.933 --> 06:56.228
Must be 45 if she's a day.

06:56.355 --> 06:59.398
She's older than my mum.

06:59.523 --> 07:01.609
Well, apart from those two
that boss me around,

07:01.733 --> 07:03.694
there's a, uh, lady
called Miss Cartwright

07:03.820 --> 07:06.655
and a Mr. Ryder
and a migrant called Magda.

07:06.781 --> 07:09.368
"New Australians"
we're supposed to call them.

07:09.492 --> 07:11.743
The other two don't like her.
Why not?

07:11.869 --> 07:14.079
I don't know,
they think she's a bossy boots.

07:14.206 --> 07:15.747
Bossy boots.

07:15.874 --> 07:17.501
Well, I'm off.

07:17.624 --> 07:19.293
So how'd you go, love?

07:19.419 --> 07:21.920
Good, Dad.
Lots of carrying things.

07:22.047 --> 07:23.631
Good for you.

07:23.756 --> 07:25.716
Build you up.
Have a look at you,

07:25.841 --> 07:27.844
you're like an underfed canary.

07:27.969 --> 07:30.012
All right.
See you in the morning.

07:30.138 --> 07:31.556
Bye.
Tooroo.

07:39.063 --> 07:40.565
This came for you.

07:58.040 --> 08:00.752
Oh, Dad will have to sign this.

08:00.876 --> 08:02.211
Do you think he will?

08:02.337 --> 08:03.963
We'll think of something.

08:04.089 --> 08:06.341
It's not gonna be easy,
but it's just a matter

08:06.465 --> 08:08.343
of picking the right time.
All right?

08:08.467 --> 08:10.220
Look, your dad's all right.

08:10.344 --> 08:11.970
He sometimes needs a bit of...

08:12.096 --> 08:14.140
Careful handling?

08:14.265 --> 08:15.307
Yeah.

08:40.707 --> 08:43.668
Help me with this,
will you, dear?

08:53.555 --> 08:56.265
Zip that up for me, will you?

08:59.311 --> 09:00.478
It's...

09:04.731 --> 09:08.153
I... I don't think
it's going to fit.

09:08.277 --> 09:12.573
Oh, nonsense.
I always wear this size.

09:14.241 --> 09:15.327
I don't...

09:22.208 --> 09:26.045
Undo... Undo it.
I don't... I'm sorry.

09:26.170 --> 09:29.591
What did you do?
I...

09:31.968 --> 09:34.345
Now, now, Mrs. Wentworth,
do try to hold still.

09:34.471 --> 09:36.848
You better clear off,
we'll sort this out.

09:36.972 --> 09:38.517
I'm sorry.

09:38.641 --> 09:40.685
I can see the problem.

09:40.809 --> 09:43.479
Oh, Mrs. Wentworth,
we've had so much

09:43.605 --> 09:45.147
trouble with zippers lately.

09:45.273 --> 09:48.193
They make them in Melbourne,
or Japan or somewhere.

09:49.486 --> 09:52.530
Oh, there.
Oh, thank you.

09:54.699 --> 09:57.952
What happened?
The new assistant.

10:02.039 --> 10:04.750
I know it may be
a bit unfair, but perhaps

10:04.876 --> 10:06.877
we should dismiss
that young girl.

10:07.003 --> 10:09.755
Mrs. Wentworth will be
on the warpath.

10:09.880 --> 10:11.966
Her family have shares
in Goode's, you know.

10:12.090 --> 10:14.343
Oh, I don't think she will
make a fuss, Mr. Ryder.

10:14.468 --> 10:16.596
She'll be a laughingstock
if word gets around

10:16.720 --> 10:19.057
about what size dress
she's trying to squeeze into.

10:19.182 --> 10:22.851
And word could get around,
I suppose, Miss Cartwright.

10:22.977 --> 10:25.063
It could, Mr. Ryder.

10:25.187 --> 10:26.730
It could.

10:35.947 --> 10:37.241
You don't like the lunch room?

10:37.367 --> 10:39.285
Oh, not too much.

10:39.410 --> 10:41.287
It's nicer out here.

10:41.412 --> 10:42.872
Must be a good book.

10:42.996 --> 10:44.748
Oh, it is.

10:44.874 --> 10:46.792
It's wonderful.
I'm just finishing it.

10:46.917 --> 10:48.378
You can borrow it if you like.

10:49.671 --> 10:52.257
It's pretty big, I don't know.

10:52.382 --> 10:53.382
What's it called?

10:53.465 --> 10:55.426
<i>Anna Karenina.</i>

10:55.552 --> 10:57.761
Is it a romance?
I like romances.

10:59.263 --> 11:01.682
Well, sort of.

11:01.807 --> 11:04.352
Come on, we'd better get back.
Oh, yes.

11:04.476 --> 11:07.606
It's... It's by Tolstoy.
It's set in Russia.

11:07.730 --> 11:09.315
Russia?
Yeah.

11:09.440 --> 11:10.649
You can't read Russian.

11:10.774 --> 11:13.194
Oh, no.
No, it's a translation.

11:13.318 --> 11:14.862
- Oh.
- Yeah.

11:14.986 --> 11:16.947
Sorry, but the fact is
that when I got it home

11:17.073 --> 11:19.116
and tried it on,
it wasn't really my color.

11:19.241 --> 11:20.910
It's just not me.
I see...

11:21.035 --> 11:23.913
Oh, Lisa, those go by the lift.
Okay.

11:24.037 --> 11:26.415
And what about the sales books
I asked you to check?

11:26.541 --> 11:27.958
You add the totals page-by-page,

11:28.084 --> 11:29.668
then compare them
with the receipts.

11:29.835 --> 11:31.003
Yes, I've done that.

11:32.338 --> 11:34.881
Miss? My dress?

11:35.007 --> 11:38.553
Yes, well, um,
you can exchange it, madam,

11:38.677 --> 11:40.971
but not if it's been worn.

11:41.097 --> 11:42.724
It hasn't been worn.

11:42.848 --> 11:45.476
Very well. Third floor
for exchanges, madam.

11:48.186 --> 11:50.105
Liar.
Of course.

11:52.858 --> 11:56.570
Oh, look out. Here she comes.
Miss Croatia 1938.

11:57.655 --> 11:59.615
Ladies.

11:59.741 --> 12:01.116
Today is the day I am stealing

12:01.241 --> 12:03.410
your little schoolgirl slave
for a few hours.

12:03.536 --> 12:04.787
Oh, no. No, you're not.

12:04.912 --> 12:06.830
Can't you see that
we're run off our feet?

12:06.955 --> 12:09.333
Quite so, but we are all
our feet run off.

12:09.459 --> 12:12.044
But Miss Cartwright has said
that we can keep Lisa...

12:12.169 --> 12:14.339
I have cleared the matter
with Miss Cartwright.

12:14.463 --> 12:15.965
Come, Lisa.

12:16.089 --> 12:19.426
I will show you how we do things
in Model Gowns.

12:19.552 --> 12:21.052
Come.

12:24.556 --> 12:25.932
Well, bugger that.

12:26.058 --> 12:28.143
She knew we needed
the extra help this week.

12:28.269 --> 12:29.770
Typical.
Thinks she owns the place.

12:29.895 --> 12:31.146
Yeah, typical.

12:31.272 --> 12:33.274
Do you have this
in any other colors?

12:33.398 --> 12:35.025
Certainly, madam.

12:37.737 --> 12:40.365
These frocks are all unique,

12:40.489 --> 12:43.702
and are all only
by the best designers.

12:43.826 --> 12:46.329
This, for example,
is Hardy Amies.

12:46.453 --> 12:49.707
A customer wearing
one of these creations

12:49.833 --> 12:52.584
knows that she will not
meet another wearing the same

12:52.711 --> 12:55.755
which is so terrible a thing
to happen to a woman.

12:55.878 --> 12:59.634
You might find the same frock
at George's in Melbourne,

12:59.758 --> 13:02.261
but who goes to Melbourne? Ha.

13:02.386 --> 13:04.929
And we do not keep
different sizes

13:05.056 --> 13:06.640
of the same model because...?

13:06.766 --> 13:09.142
Then the frock
wouldn't be unique?

13:09.267 --> 13:11.102
Quite so.

13:11.228 --> 13:14.940
This is perfect for one
of our younger society matrons,

13:15.065 --> 13:19.111
perhaps to attend
a ball at the Trocadero.

13:19.236 --> 13:21.656
It is by Elizabeth Arden.

13:21.781 --> 13:25.200
The Americans are so modern.

13:25.326 --> 13:27.494
This...

13:27.620 --> 13:31.582
I would like for myself,
it is so very well-cut.

13:31.707 --> 13:35.293
See the detail. But I am not
at my best in the English style.

13:35.420 --> 13:37.004
I cannot understand it,

13:37.128 --> 13:39.340
but all English women
are made in shape of a pear.

13:39.464 --> 13:40.884
Now, the French.

13:41.008 --> 13:42.635
Jacques Fath,

13:42.760 --> 13:44.512
Chanel, the great Dior,

13:44.636 --> 13:47.556
cut to fit a woman
with bosoms and hips,

13:47.681 --> 13:51.894
but they make her look slim
nevertheless, that is artistry.

13:52.019 --> 13:54.898
I suppose
these are very expensive.

13:55.021 --> 13:57.107
Ha! Expensive?

13:57.233 --> 13:59.276
They are fantastically
expensive.

13:59.401 --> 14:01.862
And such cost

14:01.986 --> 14:05.490
as you may one day appreciate

14:05.617 --> 14:07.951
is part of the charm.

14:11.288 --> 14:13.582
Model Gowns?

14:13.707 --> 14:17.002
Well, I thought they were
all model gowns at Goode's.

14:17.128 --> 14:19.797
Oh, no, most of them
are un-model gowns.

14:19.923 --> 14:22.675
They come in all sizes,
and anyone can buy them.

14:22.800 --> 14:24.676
Anyway, no one
has the same clothes

14:24.803 --> 14:26.471
you do because I make them.

14:26.595 --> 14:28.263
So they're unique too,
aren't they?

14:30.641 --> 14:32.769
Just think of that
lovely pink frock I made you.

14:32.893 --> 14:34.102
The one with the frills.

14:34.227 --> 14:35.939
That's right, Mum.

14:36.062 --> 14:37.981
Well, I thought
you liked it, Lesley.

14:38.106 --> 14:39.733
Oh, I... I do, Mum.

14:39.859 --> 14:42.986
I was just thinking that most
of the Model Gowns are

14:43.112 --> 14:44.905
evening frocks.

14:45.030 --> 14:46.282
Oh.

14:46.407 --> 14:49.619
Oh, well, that's another story.

14:51.119 --> 14:52.955
Well, I suppose
I could try my hand

14:53.079 --> 14:55.958
at that if you wanted
to go to a ball.

14:56.083 --> 14:58.877
Oh, you'll look just lovely.
You would.

14:59.003 --> 15:01.046
Well, thanks, Mum.

15:02.840 --> 15:06.092
♪ Volare ♪

15:06.218 --> 15:08.554
♪ Whoa ♪

15:09.806 --> 15:12.891
♪ Cantare ♪

15:13.017 --> 15:15.435
♪ Whoa ♪

15:15.560 --> 15:19.147
♪ Let's fly way up
To the clouds ♪

15:19.273 --> 15:23.736
♪ Away from
The maddening crowds ♪

15:23.862 --> 15:28.573
♪ Volare, whoa... ♪

15:28.700 --> 15:31.995
<i>The first transpacific service flown in.</i>

15:37.624 --> 15:40.502
Patty? I'm home.

15:43.171 --> 15:44.381
<i>Now our sports.</i>

15:44.506 --> 15:47.342
What's for tea?
Steak.

15:48.511 --> 15:50.095
Steak again?

15:50.221 --> 15:51.764
Yeah, it's all you ever want.

15:51.889 --> 15:53.808
Got you chops,
you said it was all bone.

15:53.932 --> 15:57.061
Yeah, too right it was.

16:00.063 --> 16:01.482
I'm done in.

16:01.607 --> 16:03.860
Been on a roof all day.

16:03.984 --> 16:07.612
Bloody hot.
We got some beer?

16:07.739 --> 16:10.240
Yup. In the fridge.

16:16.287 --> 16:18.207
<i>For the lovely run.</i>

16:18.331 --> 16:19.709
<i>Down towards the line.</i>

16:19.834 --> 16:21.753
<i>Kira went into the lead
from My Fingers.</i>

16:21.877 --> 16:24.128
<i>Just right around,
and here's Galilee!</i>

16:24.255 --> 16:27.382
<i>Galilee is pulling up close.</i>
Come on, get up, get up.

16:27.507 --> 16:30.761
Not against the rail.

16:30.887 --> 16:32.930
Get out of there.

16:33.056 --> 16:34.807
Come on, there you go,
there you go.

16:34.932 --> 16:37.475
<i>Half the run to go, Galilee is racing away,</i>

16:37.602 --> 16:39.228
<i>and the win, easily.
I think...</i>

16:39.353 --> 16:41.647
Yeah. Beauty.

16:44.107 --> 16:46.902
Roughly won that side, huh?

16:47.027 --> 16:48.987
Won a few quid on that, heh.

16:49.113 --> 16:50.782
Dad?
Yeah?

16:50.906 --> 16:52.824
Dad, I need you
to sign this form.

16:52.951 --> 16:54.535
It's for a Commonwealth
scholarship.

16:54.660 --> 16:56.245
If I get it,
it means the government

16:56.369 --> 16:58.456
will pay my fees
to Sydney University.

16:58.581 --> 16:59.749
It's just...
Just there.

16:59.874 --> 17:01.249
Look, I've told you before.

17:01.375 --> 17:03.175
No daughter of mine...
Is going to university.

17:03.251 --> 17:04.836
That's right.
I know, but...

17:04.963 --> 17:06.838
Ed, she might not get the scholarship.

17:06.965 --> 17:08.131
Just sign it for her school.

17:08.257 --> 17:09.800
Just for their records,
that's all.

17:09.926 --> 17:11.803
Here we go, we go.
Ed?

17:11.928 --> 17:13.220
Please, Dad.

17:13.344 --> 17:15.180
<i>They're racing and rising...</i>

17:15.305 --> 17:18.017
Please, Dad.
Just sign it, Ed.

17:18.141 --> 17:20.269
Just for their records,
that's all.

17:20.394 --> 17:22.605
Just... Just, Dad, please.
Ed?

17:22.729 --> 17:24.148
Give a man a break, will you?

17:24.273 --> 17:25.483
Just for their records.

17:27.234 --> 17:29.361
All right, there you go,
there you go.

17:31.195 --> 17:33.865
Go on, go on.
Oh, no, he's off the bit.

17:33.992 --> 17:35.492
Get up.

17:35.618 --> 17:37.244
Oh, you...

17:37.368 --> 17:39.412
I knew I shouldn't
have put it on the nose.

17:41.665 --> 17:43.291
University?

17:43.417 --> 17:45.962
You didn't see me going
to any bloody university.

17:46.087 --> 17:48.171
And I turned out
all right, didn't I?

17:48.297 --> 17:52.009
♪ Ooh, ooh ♪

17:52.134 --> 17:55.303
♪ Ooh, ooh ♪

17:55.429 --> 17:59.808
♪ Short black hair makes him
Look so sweet ♪

17:59.933 --> 18:03.562
♪ My baby's pretty
From his head to his feet ♪

18:03.688 --> 18:07.649
♪ He is my baby
And I want you to know ♪

18:07.775 --> 18:10.736
♪ I'll only keep him
'Cause I love him so ♪

18:10.861 --> 18:12.989
♪ 'Cause he's my baby ♪

18:13.114 --> 18:15.740
♪ Wanna keep him for my own ♪

18:15.866 --> 18:18.076
I'm telling you, it was Bradman.

18:18.202 --> 18:20.579
If Keith Miller had
concentrated on his batting

18:20.704 --> 18:23.499
instead of being an all-rounder,
he'd been the best of them all.

18:23.623 --> 18:25.710
Buggar Bradman
and all that. Ugh.

18:25.834 --> 18:27.251
Keith Miller.

18:31.007 --> 18:32.799
Same again, Myra?
G and T.

18:32.925 --> 18:36.137
Fay?
I'm fine, thanks.

18:36.260 --> 18:39.473
Well, I gotta see a man
about a dog.

18:42.767 --> 18:43.810
What do you think of him?

18:43.936 --> 18:45.436
Myra, he's bloody awful.

18:45.563 --> 18:47.857
He's talked about nothing
but cricket for an hour.

18:47.981 --> 18:49.192
Come on, he's not so bad.

18:49.315 --> 18:51.026
Ray says that
he sells more insurance

18:51.152 --> 18:52.610
than anybody around Parramatta.

18:52.737 --> 18:53.988
And he's got a new car.

18:54.113 --> 18:56.239
I don't care about his car.

18:56.365 --> 18:58.116
He'll get more pissed,
and he'll crawl

18:58.242 --> 18:59.869
all over me
like a mountain goat.

18:59.994 --> 19:01.411
I'm sorry.
I'm doing my best.

19:01.537 --> 19:03.705
You're the one wanted
to meet some nice blokes.

19:03.831 --> 19:04.832
I know, I know.

19:04.956 --> 19:06.709
I do, I just...

19:08.210 --> 19:09.794
These blokes,
I don't... I don't...

19:09.920 --> 19:11.213
Fay. Come off it.

19:11.338 --> 19:13.381
These aren't bad blokes, really.

19:13.506 --> 19:14.884
I mean, what do you expect?

19:15.008 --> 19:16.300
They're gonna kiss your hand

19:16.426 --> 19:18.512
and throw their coats
over drains?

19:23.516 --> 19:26.770
♪ He's my little baby
And he's here to stay ♪

19:26.896 --> 19:29.106
♪ 'Cause he's my baby ♪

19:29.232 --> 19:31.733
♪ I wanna keep him for my own ♪

19:42.286 --> 19:43.829
Use all the hot water,
Miss Baines,

19:43.954 --> 19:45.790
there will be none left
for anyone else.

19:45.914 --> 19:47.624
I didn't use it all,
Mrs. Fairbrother.

19:47.750 --> 19:49.292
I hope not.

19:59.970 --> 20:02.222
<i>Isn't this wonderful, Bob, darling?</i>

20:02.347 --> 20:04.058
<i>Yep. Hunky-dory.</i>

20:04.182 --> 20:05.684
<i>I just love to dance with you.</i>

20:05.809 --> 20:08.145
<i>I'd like to keep on
dancing with you forever.</i>

20:08.271 --> 20:10.688
<i>Wouldn't it be wonderful
if we just kept on dancing,</i>

20:10.815 --> 20:13.399
<i>- and never did anything else?
- We'd sure get tired.</i>

20:13.525 --> 20:15.820
<i>Bob, you're so funny.</i>

20:15.944 --> 20:17.487
<i>I can feel the muscles
in your arms.</i>

20:17.612 --> 20:18.948
<i>I bet you could pick me up</i>

20:19.073 --> 20:20.341
<i>and carry me
as if I were a feather.</i>

20:20.365 --> 20:22.159
<i>I could carry 20 like you.</i>

20:22.285 --> 20:24.368
<i>I don't want you to carry 20, just me.</i>

20:24.495 --> 20:26.247
<i>Oh, Bob, I'd wish
you'd carry me off</i>

20:26.370 --> 20:28.248
<i>to some unknown green island.</i>

20:28.372 --> 20:31.710
<i>Somewhere with palms and coral
and mermaids and harps.</i>

20:31.835 --> 20:33.921
<i>Wouldn't you just love it, Bob?</i>

20:42.847 --> 20:44.222
Thank you.

20:44.348 --> 20:46.850
I drink this only
until they learn

20:46.976 --> 20:49.687
how to make coffee in Australia.

20:59.071 --> 21:00.780
Lisa.

21:06.119 --> 21:08.247
You've fallen in love.

21:12.043 --> 21:14.295
It's... It's different.

21:14.460 --> 21:17.006
It's younger designer,
but it's also too...

21:17.130 --> 21:19.967
Too small for these
big Australian girls.

21:20.091 --> 21:21.634
How much is it?

21:21.759 --> 21:24.138
One hundred and 50 guineas.

21:25.723 --> 21:27.932
If we do not sell it
before Christmas,

21:28.059 --> 21:30.228
it will go on sale
at 75 guineas.

21:30.353 --> 21:33.898
Seventy-five guineas.

21:36.649 --> 21:39.111
There will be more dresses.

21:47.953 --> 21:50.497
Well, I don't see any
problem, Mrs. Williams.

21:50.621 --> 21:52.874
You seem completely healthy.

21:52.999 --> 21:55.669
Perhaps it's your husband.
My husband?

21:55.793 --> 21:57.588
Get him to come in
for an examination.

21:57.713 --> 22:00.549
Maybe it's him who's...
No, he wouldn't like that.

22:00.673 --> 22:02.384
No.

22:02.509 --> 22:03.844
Well...

22:05.721 --> 22:09.392
tell me. How often
do you have relations?

22:09.515 --> 22:10.892
Relations?

22:11.018 --> 22:12.185
Hmm.

22:14.145 --> 22:15.772
Not that often.

22:15.898 --> 22:18.691
He's tired. Sometimes.

22:20.361 --> 22:22.612
Perhaps that's the problem.

22:22.738 --> 22:26.074
Without proximity,

22:26.199 --> 22:28.577
there's no chance of...

22:31.580 --> 22:34.332
Could you bring me,
please, the metal?

22:34.457 --> 22:36.001
The black square.

22:36.127 --> 22:37.835
Oh, yes. Right.
I think it's that way.

22:45.510 --> 22:48.471
Beautiful cocktail dress.

22:48.596 --> 22:51.976
The changing rooms are just...
Just behind you. Just there.

22:56.438 --> 22:59.482
Oh, bloody hell.
What a day.

23:01.234 --> 23:02.860
You did all right, kid.

23:02.986 --> 23:05.238
Didn't she?

23:05.364 --> 23:08.490
Yes. She did all right,
all right.

23:10.410 --> 23:12.703
You're not buying something
from Goode's, are you?

23:12.830 --> 23:13.831
It's too expensive.

23:13.955 --> 23:15.999
Staff discount.
What is it?

23:16.125 --> 23:19.420
Oh, just a little thing.
Thought it might suit me.

23:21.380 --> 23:23.131
Well, I never.

23:57.416 --> 23:58.792
Frank.

23:58.916 --> 24:01.794
I didn't hear you come in.
You're early.

24:01.920 --> 24:04.755
Boss said to knock off early
to do some Christmas shopping.

24:04.882 --> 24:06.757
Why are you dressed for bed?

24:06.884 --> 24:08.511
It's only 6:00.

24:08.634 --> 24:11.221
It's new, the nightdress.

24:11.347 --> 24:13.307
I was just trying it on.

24:13.432 --> 24:16.767
It didn't cost much,
not with the staff discount.

24:16.894 --> 24:18.645
I'll take it off now.

24:18.769 --> 24:20.438
I'll help you...

24:22.940 --> 24:24.566
take it off.

24:50.592 --> 24:55.932
<i>Tyger Tyger, burning bright,
In the forests of the night;</i>

24:56.058 --> 24:58.184
<i>What immortal hand or eye</i>

24:58.310 --> 25:00.979
<i>Could frame
thy fearful symmetry?</i>

25:01.104 --> 25:03.731
<i>In what distant deeps or skies.</i>

25:03.857 --> 25:06.859
<i>Burnt the fire of thine eyes?</i>

25:06.984 --> 25:10.030
<i>On what wings dare he aspire?</i>

25:10.154 --> 25:13.366
<i>What the hand,
dare seize the fire?</i>

25:22.250 --> 25:23.626
Crikey, where have you been?

25:23.752 --> 25:25.628
We're busier than
a one-armed bricklayer.

25:25.753 --> 25:28.591
Mrs. Williams, Christmas isn't
the best time for lateness.

25:28.714 --> 25:30.716
Sorry, Miss Cartwright,
the alarm didn't go off.

25:30.842 --> 25:32.385
It's the first time
I've been late

25:32.511 --> 25:33.928
since Mum's hysterectomy.

25:37.599 --> 25:39.850
Everything okay?

25:39.977 --> 25:42.854
Oh, you look like the cat
that ate the cream, I must say.

25:42.979 --> 25:46.150
Everything's fine.
I just overslept.

25:46.274 --> 25:48.650
Thank you.

25:48.777 --> 25:50.653
You're with us today, remember?

25:50.779 --> 25:53.156
No sneaking back
to Dracula's hideout.

26:22.726 --> 26:25.021
I just thought he might
have got a bit of overtime.

26:25.147 --> 26:26.648
He's not usually late.

26:26.773 --> 26:28.608
<i>He called up this afternoon.</i>

26:28.733 --> 26:31.152
<i>Said he didn't feel too good.</i>

26:31.277 --> 26:33.779
- Oh, well...
<i>- Look, don't worry, love.</i>

26:33.904 --> 26:35.739
<i>There'll be some explanation.</i>

26:35.865 --> 26:38.619
<i>He's probably down at the pub
with a few mates.</i>

26:52.131 --> 26:54.008
Excuse me.

26:54.134 --> 26:55.134
Thank you.

27:02.433 --> 27:04.603
- You will come?
- Yes, heh.

27:07.147 --> 27:09.232
Lisa.

27:09.357 --> 27:11.901
Is the beast from the Balkans
trying to steal you again?

27:12.027 --> 27:13.696
No, she's invited me to lunch

27:13.819 --> 27:16.240
on Saturday
with her and her husband.

27:16.365 --> 27:17.366
She's got a husband?

27:17.490 --> 27:19.243
Well, evidently.

27:19.367 --> 27:21.203
Well, I hope you like
frogs' legs

27:21.327 --> 27:23.287
and fish eyes
and sheep's testicles

27:23.414 --> 27:26.333
because those reffos eat
all kinds of horrible things.

27:26.458 --> 27:29.793
Oh, find me a right foot,
will you? Thank you.

28:07.831 --> 28:09.584
Isn't it glorious?

28:09.710 --> 28:12.337
Mm-hmm.

28:12.462 --> 28:14.796
It's just lovely.
Hmm.

28:16.550 --> 28:18.635
I am happy.

28:18.761 --> 28:21.346
Be happy. Always.

28:21.472 --> 28:23.139
It's good choice.

28:32.356 --> 28:37.946
Please, may I present
my husband, Stefan Szombathelyi.

28:38.071 --> 28:41.992
He's a Hungarian
but not, alas, a count.

28:42.116 --> 28:45.870
But you can't have everything.
Stefan, this is Lisa Miles.

28:45.995 --> 28:51.669
My most capable junior
temporary sales assistant.

28:51.793 --> 28:53.712
I'm enchanted to meet you.

28:53.836 --> 28:56.548
Oh.
Lisa.

28:56.673 --> 28:58.424
I suppose you haven't heard,

28:58.550 --> 29:00.135
we Europeans,
we are always kissing.

29:03.347 --> 29:05.515
Some wine?
We've been amazing to discover

29:05.641 --> 29:07.433
very fine Australian wine.

29:07.559 --> 29:09.811
Um...
Oh, dear, perhaps some lemonade.

29:09.936 --> 29:13.774
Oh, lemonade, yes.
Thank you.

29:13.898 --> 29:17.653
So, Lisa, tell me,
do you like to read novels?

29:17.778 --> 29:20.738
Oh, yes, I do. I just
finished<i> Anna Karenina.</i>

29:20.863 --> 29:22.448
I haven't decided
what to read next.

29:22.574 --> 29:24.033
There are so many
to choose from.

29:24.159 --> 29:26.076
Oh, how true. And the number
always grows,

29:26.203 --> 29:27.370
I assure you.
Thank you.

29:27.496 --> 29:28.621
Perhaps your next choice

29:28.747 --> 29:30.749
should be something
quite different.

29:30.873 --> 29:33.293
Hmm.<i> Emma,</i>
have you read that yet?

29:33.418 --> 29:34.628
No. I haven't.

29:34.752 --> 29:36.086
Oh, well, that is settled, then.

29:36.213 --> 29:39.466
Jane Austen, I assure you,
is as great a genius

29:39.591 --> 29:42.343
as Tolstoy, whatever they say.

29:42.469 --> 29:44.554
Let me have your opinion
in due course, yes?

29:44.680 --> 29:46.472
Yes, I will.

29:46.597 --> 29:48.432
Oh, that will be Rudi.

29:48.558 --> 29:51.186
Such a sense of timing.
Like no one.

29:52.770 --> 29:55.690
Yes.
Lisa, put more<i> torokméz.</i>

29:55.816 --> 29:56.899
More?
Yes.

30:01.779 --> 30:03.823
Ah. Lunchtime.

30:03.948 --> 30:06.784
I have such a sense of timing.
Like no one.

30:06.910 --> 30:09.370
But I have brought a cake.

30:12.248 --> 30:15.125
So tell us Rudi, we've
been discussing Jane Austen.

30:15.251 --> 30:16.878
Ah.
What do you think of her?

30:17.045 --> 30:20.047
Well, my opinion
has yet to be formed,

30:20.173 --> 30:22.133
as I have not read one word.

30:22.258 --> 30:23.552
Oh! A philistine.

30:23.676 --> 30:25.928
No, the truth is

30:26.054 --> 30:28.180
I am rather infatuated
with Charles Dickens.

30:28.306 --> 30:31.058
You see, he's much better in
English than he is in Hungarian.

30:31.183 --> 30:35.230
So I am reading again
what I read so long ago.

30:35.354 --> 30:39.818
Dickens in Serbo-Croat,
I never read. And I don't care.

30:39.942 --> 30:42.445
No, his books
are stupendously long.

30:42.570 --> 30:44.907
What was he thinking?
I don't have time.

30:45.031 --> 30:47.910
Magda prefers<i> Vogue.</i>

30:48.033 --> 30:51.455
<i>Vogue</i> and Agatha Christie too.

30:51.579 --> 30:53.581
That's true.
Tell me, what do you think?

30:53.707 --> 30:55.041
- Hmm?
- Of the cake.

30:55.165 --> 30:56.876
Oh, it's wonderful.

30:57.001 --> 30:59.671
I must say that in Melbourne,
where I have been living,

30:59.796 --> 31:01.672
there are better cake
shops than Sydney.

31:01.798 --> 31:03.884
In Melbourne,
they have more need of cake,

31:04.007 --> 31:05.927
having more or less,
nothing else.

31:18.482 --> 31:21.067
Mum?
So, what's going on?

31:21.191 --> 31:22.526
Out with it.

31:22.653 --> 31:24.821
Frank's disappeared.

31:24.947 --> 31:26.781
I knew something was up.

31:26.907 --> 31:29.284
Since when?

31:29.408 --> 31:32.871
He didn't come home on Thursday.
He wasn't at work on Friday.

31:32.995 --> 31:35.165
They don't know
where he is, either.

31:35.289 --> 31:37.500
I called the police,
and they said not to worry.

31:37.626 --> 31:39.252
They said
people do it all the time.

31:39.377 --> 31:41.922
That I should call
if he's not back in a week.

31:42.045 --> 31:45.216
- You two have a fight?
- No.

31:45.341 --> 31:47.635
Well, I don't know.

31:47.761 --> 31:50.639
He was always a bit
on the peculiar side.

31:50.764 --> 31:52.556
I know.
You and Dawn

31:52.683 --> 31:55.476
and Joy, you didn't...

31:55.602 --> 31:56.686
You don't like him.

31:56.812 --> 31:58.271
I've never said that.

31:59.857 --> 32:01.899
But you wouldn't
call him a livewire.

32:02.859 --> 32:05.362
He's just shy. That's all.

32:05.486 --> 32:08.071
Growing up on the farm, he
didn't have anyone to talk to.

32:08.197 --> 32:09.740
He's always been very...

32:09.867 --> 32:12.409
inhibited.
Listen, Patty.

32:12.536 --> 32:14.328
I'll tell you this.

32:14.454 --> 32:16.914
No one understands men.

32:17.040 --> 32:18.248
We don't understand them,

32:18.375 --> 32:20.961
and they don't
understand themselves.

32:21.084 --> 32:24.172
They do these stupid things.

32:24.297 --> 32:29.177
But they always come back,
in the end.

32:29.301 --> 32:32.096
They can't really manage
by themselves, men can't.

32:34.139 --> 32:36.058
He'll be back.

32:37.476 --> 32:40.062
Or he'll have me to answer to.

32:43.858 --> 32:46.610
So may I? I was just...

32:46.737 --> 32:51.699
I was just wondering how it
would look if we tried this.

32:59.374 --> 33:02.084
They love to talk
in their barbaric Hungarian.

33:02.209 --> 33:05.505
I thought you were Hungarian.
Me? No.

33:05.630 --> 33:07.507
I am Slovenian.

33:07.631 --> 33:09.801
I suppose you do not know
what it is, Slovenian?

33:09.926 --> 33:11.177
Can you take it off?

33:11.301 --> 33:14.847
Yes, I do.
Slovenia is part of Yugoslavia.

33:14.973 --> 33:19.810
The capital city is Ljubljana.

33:19.937 --> 33:22.480
You are genius. No.

33:22.605 --> 33:25.067
I never met before an Australian

33:25.190 --> 33:27.944
who had even heard
of this place.

33:28.070 --> 33:30.572
Stefan and I,
we met not in Europe,

33:30.697 --> 33:33.991
but in Australia.

33:35.201 --> 33:37.871
In one of your migrant camps.

33:38.913 --> 33:41.332
Not such a lovely place,

33:41.458 --> 33:44.920
but it was paradise after
what we had been through.

33:45.044 --> 33:47.213
I see.

33:47.337 --> 33:50.550
Stefan and I, we met
learning English together.

33:52.510 --> 33:54.972
We can speak German,

33:55.097 --> 33:56.764
but it's not
our favorite language.

33:58.266 --> 34:00.602
There. It's much better.

34:00.726 --> 34:02.061
Lisa,

34:02.186 --> 34:04.439
can you see
without your glasses?

34:04.564 --> 34:07.900
Oh, yes, I only really
need them for reading.

34:08.026 --> 34:09.987
So why do you wear them always?

34:10.111 --> 34:13.697
I suppose
because I'm always reading.

34:13.823 --> 34:16.117
Can we take them off?

34:16.242 --> 34:17.452
Yes.

34:26.294 --> 34:28.171
You have beautiful eyes.

34:30.339 --> 34:32.175
So one last thing.

34:32.299 --> 34:35.218
Lipstick, I had...

34:35.345 --> 34:37.681
It's nice pink,

34:37.805 --> 34:40.099
and you can keep it.

34:40.224 --> 34:44.937
It's suitable for...
For<i> jeune fille.</i>

34:45.062 --> 34:48.858
You have so slim
a figure. Hold on.

34:50.735 --> 34:54.614
I envy you this much.
You should make the most of it.

34:54.738 --> 34:56.324
There, turn around.

34:57.826 --> 35:01.662
You look charming.

35:01.788 --> 35:04.540
And with a bit more experience,

35:04.666 --> 35:07.919
Lisa, you could be enchanting.

35:08.044 --> 35:09.921
How do you feel?

35:11.548 --> 35:12.590
Different.

35:33.152 --> 35:35.529
Hello, Mum. Look.

35:37.532 --> 35:40.701
Lesley, I thought you told us
you'd be home by 4:00?

35:40.827 --> 35:43.996
Oh, Magda gave me this
and this belt, do you like it?

35:44.122 --> 35:46.290
Well...
I'm sorry I'm late,

35:46.416 --> 35:48.500
but we went for a walk,
and Magda told me

35:48.626 --> 35:50.044
about Slovenia before the war.

35:50.169 --> 35:51.588
Oh, and we talked about books,

35:51.712 --> 35:53.215
and Stefan made us
a lovely lunch.

35:53.338 --> 35:56.050
Who's Stefan?
He's Magda's husband.

35:56.176 --> 35:58.344
Oh, right.
He made the lunch?

35:59.637 --> 36:01.056
European men like to cook.

36:01.180 --> 36:02.848
Ah. Hmm.

36:02.974 --> 36:04.350
Didn't know that.

36:04.474 --> 36:07.061
I like to drink.
I'm off to the pub.

36:07.187 --> 36:08.813
See you in a couple of hours.

36:11.982 --> 36:15.319
Lesley, I had a telephone call
from that Magda.

36:15.444 --> 36:18.990
That Mrs. Zombie
something or other,

36:19.115 --> 36:22.452
to say you're on your way,
which was very nice of her,

36:22.577 --> 36:26.998
but, um, she tried to tell me
that your name was Lisa.

36:27.124 --> 36:29.626
She didn't seem
to know your name.

36:29.750 --> 36:33.712
Oh, well, that's what
they call me at Goode's.

36:33.838 --> 36:37.883
What... What do you mean?
Your... Your name's Lesley.

36:38.008 --> 36:40.053
But I don't like Lesley.

36:40.177 --> 36:43.430
But that's your name.
Lesley.

36:43.555 --> 36:46.809
But I want to be Lisa.
And I will be. And I am.

36:46.935 --> 36:49.771
Lisa?

36:49.896 --> 36:51.064
How do you think it feels

36:51.189 --> 36:52.689
to have your own child
telling you

36:52.816 --> 36:54.316
that she wants a different name?

36:54.442 --> 36:56.319
What's wrong with Lesley?

36:56.443 --> 36:58.154
Lesley's a lovely name.

36:58.280 --> 36:59.530
Oh, Mum, I just...

36:59.656 --> 37:01.908
I just wanted
a real girl's name.

37:02.032 --> 37:03.742
Lesley is a boy's name.

37:03.868 --> 37:06.496
No, Lesley's a girl's name too.
It's spelt differently.

37:06.620 --> 37:10.874
But it sounds the same,
and that's what counts.

37:11.001 --> 37:14.170
I just wanted a proper
girl's name for when I grow up.

37:14.296 --> 37:16.131
I've been a child
for so long now,

37:16.256 --> 37:18.590
I just want to be grown-up.

37:20.177 --> 37:21.593
Oh, Lesley.

37:24.263 --> 37:28.475
If you only knew what
being grown-up can be like,

37:28.601 --> 37:30.686
you wouldn't...

37:30.812 --> 37:33.439
wish it to be
any faster than it is.

37:33.563 --> 37:35.899
Oh, Mum.

37:36.026 --> 37:38.402
Please don't cry, Mum.

37:38.527 --> 37:42.156
Yeah, I suppose I always knew
that I'd lose you some day.

37:42.282 --> 37:44.992
Oh, no, you'll never lose me.

37:45.118 --> 37:47.286
I'll stay with you always.

37:47.411 --> 37:50.081
But you can't say that,
sweetheart.

37:50.206 --> 37:52.749
Because you'll marry,
you'll go abroad.

37:52.875 --> 37:55.418
You can't stay with me always.

37:55.545 --> 37:58.256
I'm just being selfish,
I suppose.

37:58.380 --> 38:00.007
No, you're not.

38:00.133 --> 38:03.219
Even if I do marry or go away,
I'll still come and see you.

38:03.345 --> 38:05.680
Often.
Well, I hope so.

38:05.804 --> 38:07.223
Of course, I will.

38:08.807 --> 38:11.101
And you can keep on
calling me Lesley, if you like.

38:11.228 --> 38:12.353
I'll see.

38:12.478 --> 38:14.189
I might manage to call you Lisa

38:14.313 --> 38:17.191
sometimes, but that depends.

38:17.316 --> 38:19.902
Right, but now something
more important,

38:20.028 --> 38:21.153
your dad's dinner.

38:26.616 --> 38:29.371
You know, that belt
does look very nice on you.

38:29.495 --> 38:31.621
I hope you thanked
Magda properly for that.

38:31.748 --> 38:33.790
Of course, I did.

38:41.132 --> 38:44.761
Your little Australian
schoolgirl is most charming.

38:44.885 --> 38:49.891
I can see you're going to turn
a sow's ear into a silk purse.

38:50.016 --> 38:52.936
If I do a good deed
for once in my life,

38:53.060 --> 38:55.312
I cannot see the humor.

38:55.438 --> 38:57.981
Perhaps she reminds me
a little of...

38:58.108 --> 38:59.942
Of?

39:00.068 --> 39:02.778
Myself before the war.

39:02.904 --> 39:04.738
Really?

39:04.864 --> 39:07.200
I find that hard to believe.

39:07.324 --> 39:09.077
She's so...

39:09.202 --> 39:10.787
Innocent?

39:10.911 --> 39:12.705
And naive, I know.

39:14.581 --> 39:17.751
Do you think I was always
<i>trés sophistiqué?</i>

39:19.296 --> 39:21.172
Well, yes.

39:22.672 --> 39:24.633
I can assure you
that's not the case.

39:24.759 --> 39:26.927
Stefan.
Mm-hmm?

39:27.053 --> 39:29.931
This is most odd.

39:30.056 --> 39:32.766
When I called Lisa's mother,

39:32.892 --> 39:37.562
she didn't seem to know
the name of her own child.

39:37.687 --> 39:40.108
"Lesley," she pronounces it.

39:41.692 --> 39:43.485
Hmm.
This Australian speech

39:43.611 --> 39:45.070
is very bizarre.

39:45.195 --> 39:46.447
What can be expected?

39:46.572 --> 39:48.241
They're the descendants
of convicts.

39:48.365 --> 39:52.703
Their accent comes from
the lower classes of England.

39:52.829 --> 39:56.457
"Lesley. Les..."

39:56.581 --> 39:58.960
"Lesley?"

39:59.084 --> 40:01.753
"Lesley, Lesley."

40:01.879 --> 40:02.880
"Lesley."

40:23.566 --> 40:25.527
Espresso.

40:26.988 --> 40:28.364
Heh.
Ah.

40:33.869 --> 40:37.040
Oh. It's only place in town
with decent coffee.

40:37.164 --> 40:40.043
Owned by Russian reffos.

40:40.167 --> 40:42.461
A relative of the czar,
they say.

40:42.628 --> 40:45.882
From the court of St. Petersburg
to Pitt Street in Sydney.

40:46.006 --> 40:50.053
Now, I only have
20 minutes. What?

40:50.177 --> 40:53.681
Rudi needs assistance.
Oh.

40:53.805 --> 40:56.475
I wish to find a girlfriend.

40:56.600 --> 40:58.394
I know very few people
here in Sydney.

40:58.520 --> 41:00.645
I look to you
and Stefan for help.

41:00.771 --> 41:03.066
Well, I can think of no one
at the moment.

41:03.190 --> 41:05.026
I think you have
to arrange this yourself.

41:05.150 --> 41:06.818
I am not fussy.

41:06.944 --> 41:10.490
No, you only want a beauty,
less than 30 years old,

41:10.614 --> 41:13.159
cultivated,
and if not, also rich.

41:13.283 --> 41:15.327
Certainly, I will want a beauty.

41:15.453 --> 41:18.914
Cultivated, well, I have heard
there is such a thing, but...

41:19.040 --> 41:22.042
What do you mean?
Naturally, we are cultivated.

41:22.167 --> 41:25.003
We reffos, we New Australians.
We are famous for it.

41:25.128 --> 41:26.671
It's our most
despicable quality.

41:26.797 --> 41:28.631
Oh, no, you misunderstand me.

41:28.758 --> 41:31.218
This time,
I am not looking for a reffo.

41:31.344 --> 41:33.220
I want an Australian girl.

41:33.346 --> 41:35.056
An Australian?

41:36.515 --> 41:37.975
Some of them are very beautiful.

41:38.100 --> 41:38.809
Have you not noticed?

41:38.935 --> 41:40.352
Yes.

41:40.478 --> 41:42.522
The cultivated ones
have gone away.

41:42.645 --> 41:43.689
They've gone away?

41:43.814 --> 41:45.524
Mm-hmm.
Hmm.

41:45.649 --> 41:47.318
Where have they gone?

41:47.443 --> 41:50.695
They have... They have
gone away to London.

41:50.820 --> 41:53.990
Sometimes they go to Paris...

41:54.117 --> 41:56.453
To Paris or to New York, Rome.

41:56.577 --> 41:59.204
Mm.
And if you find one here,

41:59.330 --> 42:02.375
she is saving her fare,
I guarantee it.

42:02.499 --> 42:04.167
Well,

42:04.294 --> 42:07.087
then I shall take
an uncultivated one,

42:07.213 --> 42:08.297
and cultivate her myself.

42:08.422 --> 42:11.426
But a nice, strong, healthy,

42:11.550 --> 42:13.636
available Australian girl.

42:13.761 --> 42:14.886
Hmm?

42:15.012 --> 42:16.680
But I can think of no one

42:16.805 --> 42:18.681
with all of
these qualifications, it...

42:20.560 --> 42:24.646
Maybe you should give
yourself a thought, hm?

42:27.358 --> 42:28.651
Yes?

42:34.656 --> 42:36.366
What are you doing
for Christmas?

42:36.493 --> 42:37.827
Oh, I don't know.

42:37.951 --> 42:39.829
I might go to Melbourne,
to my brother's.

42:39.953 --> 42:42.706
Melbourne? I thought
you said his wife was a cow.

42:42.831 --> 42:45.167
Yeah, she is.
She doesn't do a thing.

42:45.293 --> 42:48.505
She's got legs
like tree trunks too.

42:48.629 --> 42:50.173
Why not come to
the Blue Mountains?

42:50.297 --> 42:52.091
Mum and Dad
are retired up there.

42:52.215 --> 42:54.217
Yeah, that might be nice.
Thanks.

42:54.344 --> 42:56.470
And there's always a good
New Year's Eve party

42:56.595 --> 42:58.556
at the Hydro.

42:58.681 --> 43:00.474
Oh, I forgot, you're off blokes.

43:00.600 --> 43:03.186
No, I'm not.
Just some blokes.

43:03.310 --> 43:05.438
Look, what are we gonna go to?

43:07.606 --> 43:11.402
I reckon<i> The Nun's Story.</i>
Audrey Hepburn, she's gorgeous.

43:11.527 --> 43:14.656
God, it looks serious. Can't we
see something funny? Ooh!

43:14.779 --> 43:17.241
<i>I'm All Right Jack.</i>
What about that?

43:17.366 --> 43:18.868
Ew. I don't know.

43:20.703 --> 43:23.497
<i>On The Beach,</i> Gregory Peck.
Oh, he's divine.

43:23.623 --> 43:27.001
Oh, he really is.

43:27.125 --> 43:28.586
Oh, God.

43:28.710 --> 43:33.090
♪ ...looked out
On the Feast of Stephen ♪

43:33.216 --> 43:39.347
♪ When the snow lay round about
Deep and crisp and even ♪

43:39.471 --> 43:42.807
♪ Brightly shone
The moon that night ♪

43:42.933 --> 43:45.811
♪ Though the frost was cruel ♪

43:45.935 --> 43:49.440
♪ When a poor man
Came in sight ♪

43:49.565 --> 43:53.485
♪ Gathering winter fuel ♪

44:04.162 --> 44:05.289
Magda.

44:06.666 --> 44:08.791
My mother sent you some scones.

44:08.918 --> 44:13.172
And I told her that you probably
wouldn't like them, but, um...

44:13.296 --> 44:14.757
I love scones.

44:14.882 --> 44:17.051
Oh.

44:17.175 --> 44:21.931
That is really, really kind.
Please thank your mother.

44:22.055 --> 44:28.645
Very kindly. May I wish you
and your family happy Christmas.

44:28.771 --> 44:32.775
Now, Lisa, perhaps you can
help me with a small problem.

44:32.899 --> 44:38.154
Rudi has decided that he wants
an Australian girlfriend.

44:41.617 --> 44:44.161
No. No, don't worry.
Not you.

44:44.286 --> 44:46.038
Oh, no, no.

44:46.162 --> 44:49.584
Ah, you are much too young
and too clever for him.

44:49.709 --> 44:55.297
No, someone older
and perhaps not too particular.

45:01.596 --> 45:03.846
Magda, there is someone.

45:08.935 --> 45:10.438
Oh, no. No.

45:10.562 --> 45:12.690
Not Miss Cartwright.

45:12.814 --> 45:15.610
Lisa, this is not what
he has in mind.

45:15.735 --> 45:18.361
No. No.
Not Miss Cartwright, no.

45:26.911 --> 45:28.748
Hmm.

45:29.415 --> 45:30.540
Huh.

45:31.917 --> 45:34.629
She is about 30,
more or less, not bad.

45:35.378 --> 45:37.255
She's rough.

45:37.380 --> 45:39.759
She has no style.

45:41.259 --> 45:43.012
Perfect.

45:55.148 --> 45:57.985
It's been a wonderful effort once again.

45:58.109 --> 46:00.696
Another record Christmas
for Goode's, I'm sure.

46:00.821 --> 46:04.616
Congratulations.
And thank you all very much.

46:04.742 --> 46:07.243
And happy Christmas.

46:07.370 --> 46:08.911
Happy Christmas.

46:11.414 --> 46:14.376
Well, another year gone.
Yes.

46:14.501 --> 46:18.672
My life slowly
ticks away at Goode's.

46:18.798 --> 46:20.466
Merry Christmas,
Miss Cartwright.

46:20.590 --> 46:22.217
Merry Christmas,
Mr. Ryder.

46:22.343 --> 46:25.220
I suppose you'll spend it
with your family. Oh, yes.

46:25.346 --> 46:27.931
My dear mother passed away
this year, as you know,

46:28.056 --> 46:29.851
but I have some
delightful nephews

46:29.974 --> 46:31.769
I shall spend
the festive season with.

46:37.400 --> 46:38.817
Miss Baines.

46:39.985 --> 46:41.903
Uh, I would be really charmed

46:42.028 --> 46:43.739
if you would join
my husband and I

46:43.865 --> 46:47.701
on New Year's Eve,
at our flat in Mosman.

46:48.703 --> 46:50.329
Me? New Year's Eve?

46:50.454 --> 46:52.497
There... There
will be many people,

46:52.623 --> 46:54.457
and Lisa will be there.

46:54.584 --> 46:58.254
Uh, so you will not feel
like complete stranger.

46:58.378 --> 47:00.921
Oh, oh, but I might be
in the Blue Mountains

47:01.047 --> 47:02.883
for New Year's Eve, so...

47:03.008 --> 47:05.594
So I believe they have trains.
You do your best.

47:05.720 --> 47:07.929
Here. It's address.

47:15.772 --> 47:17.898
Oh, Rudi.

47:18.023 --> 47:20.483
Here's your healthy
Australian girl.

47:20.610 --> 47:21.943
Poor lamb.

47:23.613 --> 47:25.988
<i>♪ Oh, the weather outside
Is frightful ♪</i>

47:26.115 --> 47:28.949
<i>♪ But the fire
Is so delightful ♪</i>

47:29.076 --> 47:31.952
<i>♪ And since
We've no place to go ♪</i>

47:32.079 --> 47:34.790
<i>♪ Let it snow, let it snow
Let it snow ♪</i>

47:34.916 --> 47:36.041
<i>♪ It doesn't show... ♪</i>

47:36.165 --> 47:37.709
Hi.

47:37.835 --> 47:40.378
<i>♪ ...and I brought
Some corn for popping ♪</i>

47:41.881 --> 47:43.673
How was it?
Yeah, it was good.

47:43.800 --> 47:45.216
Pretty warm
once you get out there.

47:45.342 --> 47:46.843
<i>♪ ...let it snow ♪</i>

47:46.968 --> 47:49.305
<i>♪ When we finally
Kiss good night ♪</i>

47:49.429 --> 47:52.182
<i>♪ How I'll hate going out
In the storm ♪</i>

47:52.308 --> 47:55.936
<i>♪ But if you really
Hold me tight ♪</i>

47:56.061 --> 47:58.271
<i>♪ All the way home
I'll be warm ♪</i>

47:58.397 --> 48:01.190
<i>♪ The fire is slowly dying ♪</i>

48:01.317 --> 48:04.193
<i>♪ And, my dear
We're still goodbye-ing ♪</i>

48:04.320 --> 48:06.321
<i>♪ But as long as you
Love me so ♪</i>

48:06.446 --> 48:08.698
All right, Lesley, you're
champing at the bit,

48:08.825 --> 48:10.743
so away you go.
Okay.

48:10.868 --> 48:13.495
Um, this one is for you, Mum.

48:13.620 --> 48:14.956
Thank you, sweetheart.

48:15.080 --> 48:17.625
And, Dad, I believe
this one is for you.

48:17.749 --> 48:20.042
Oh, thanks, love.
All right.

48:20.169 --> 48:23.547
And so this one must be for me.

48:23.672 --> 48:25.256
Yes, the big one's yours.

48:25.382 --> 48:27.008
Great.

48:33.389 --> 48:34.389
"Lisa"?

48:36.101 --> 48:38.855
Oh, they smell beautiful.
Thank you.

48:38.979 --> 48:41.230
No worries, Mum.
Thank you, Lisa.

48:41.357 --> 48:44.318
Ah.<i> "The Australian</i>
<i>Bloodhorse."</i>

48:44.442 --> 48:46.402
It's great, love.
Thank you.

48:46.529 --> 48:47.822
It's great.

48:47.947 --> 48:50.115
Oh...

48:53.201 --> 48:54.704
What? Don't you like it?

48:54.828 --> 48:57.999
Of course I do, Mum.

48:58.122 --> 49:00.292
Um, I was just wondering
about the color.

49:00.416 --> 49:03.962
Oh, but you've always loved pink
and these frills.

49:04.129 --> 49:05.588
Ever since you were
a little girl,

49:05.714 --> 49:06.757
you've loved them.

49:08.884 --> 49:10.928
Yes. Yes, that's right, Mum.

49:11.052 --> 49:13.429
It's terrific. Really.
Oh, good.

49:13.556 --> 49:16.683
I suppose you'll both be wanting
something from me now.

49:16.809 --> 49:19.352
It's a bit hard for me
to go Christmas shopping

49:19.478 --> 49:22.648
when I work all night
and sleep all day.

49:22.773 --> 49:24.107
Hang on.

49:24.232 --> 49:25.441
What have I got here?

49:27.151 --> 49:30.030
Come over here, doll.
There you go, sweetheart.

49:30.155 --> 49:32.449
Thanks, Dad.
Pleasure.

49:32.574 --> 49:33.700
Sweetheart?

49:35.411 --> 49:38.246
Thanks, Ed.
No worries.

49:38.372 --> 49:41.374
Well, what do you say
we go down to Manly for a swim

49:41.499 --> 49:42.751
before we go to Auntie Gert's?

49:42.877 --> 49:44.169
Mm.
Huh?

49:44.293 --> 49:45.796
What do you reckon?
Sounds good?

49:45.921 --> 49:47.630
Sounds good to me.

49:47.757 --> 49:49.632
Go! Go! Go!

49:51.259 --> 49:53.345
You won.

49:53.469 --> 49:54.804
Yes!
Oh!

49:56.723 --> 50:00.227
Right. "The pessimist sees
difficulty in every opportunity,

50:00.351 --> 50:03.312
the optimist sees opportunity
in every difficulty."

50:03.439 --> 50:04.898
Very true.
Beautiful.

50:05.024 --> 50:07.359
Oh, come on, Pat.
What's it say?

50:10.028 --> 50:12.155
"Laugh and the world
laughs with you.

50:12.281 --> 50:14.533
Weep and you weep alone."

50:15.992 --> 50:17.787
What's wrong with Auntie Patty?

50:17.911 --> 50:19.663
I think she's crying.
Patty.

50:19.788 --> 50:21.121
Is she really?
Pat?

50:22.749 --> 50:24.627
Auntie Patty's not feeling well.

50:26.044 --> 50:27.546
Bad luck,
Frank having to be away.

50:27.670 --> 50:29.881
He's a bit of a dark horse,
that Frank.

50:30.006 --> 50:32.676
Frank's not a dark horse.
Frank's a dill.

50:32.801 --> 50:34.971
At least we know one thing.
What?

50:35.095 --> 50:38.389
It couldn't be another woman.
He's no Casanova.

50:48.692 --> 50:50.569
Come on, Mother.

50:50.693 --> 50:53.362
Just a little bit
of Christmas cake.

50:54.490 --> 50:56.533
I got it at Goode's.

51:05.918 --> 51:07.418
Come on.

51:11.423 --> 51:13.759
- Take another one.
- It's all right.

51:18.054 --> 51:19.598
So I think I...

51:22.308 --> 51:25.979
I would like to propose a toast
to this country.

51:26.105 --> 51:29.107
I still cannot believe my fate.

51:29.231 --> 51:31.568
To the Commonwealth
of Australia.

51:31.694 --> 51:34.155
To Australia.

51:43.956 --> 51:46.667
It's so beautiful here.
It really is.

51:46.791 --> 51:49.210
Are you happy?
Oh, of course not.

51:49.335 --> 51:51.505
What a very vulgar suggestion.

51:51.629 --> 51:56.010
Are you?
Oh, dear. I hope not.

52:07.479 --> 52:09.856
- Here she is now.
- Oh, here we go.

52:09.982 --> 52:15.070
I'm not saying that I'm going.
I'm just thinking about it.

52:15.196 --> 52:18.199
It's a bloody mad idea.
What's that, dear?

52:18.322 --> 52:21.035
Fay's thinking about going
to a New Year's do in Sydney.

52:21.160 --> 52:22.577
Oh, that's a bit
of a trip, dear.

52:22.703 --> 52:24.829
Thank you.

52:24.955 --> 52:27.290
We're going to the Hydro,
remember?

52:27.415 --> 52:29.877
Who's gonna be at Magda's party?
Are you meeting someone?

52:30.001 --> 52:31.795
No.
I won't know anyone.

52:31.921 --> 52:34.048
I think
they'll all be continentals.

52:34.173 --> 52:37.093
Continentals?
Gosh, it's getting worse.

52:37.217 --> 52:38.635
They'll all be jabbering on,

52:38.760 --> 52:40.469
you won't know what
they're saying.

52:42.556 --> 52:45.683
One of them might be a count.
You never know.

52:45.809 --> 52:47.394
A count?
Yeah, like...

52:47.519 --> 52:49.979
I wouldn't count on that.
I mean like Count Vronsky.

52:50.106 --> 52:52.190
What's wrong with Australians?

52:58.113 --> 53:00.449
MAGDA?
So, Lisa, please pay attention.

53:00.573 --> 53:03.410
Because of the New Year parties,

53:03.534 --> 53:07.372
a lot of ladies will be
coming in, asking for dresses

53:07.496 --> 53:11.417
at the sales prices
before the sales.

53:11.543 --> 53:14.171
And you'll let them have them?
Not on your life.

53:14.336 --> 53:18.675
But, in Model Gowns,
always polite.

53:18.800 --> 53:21.637
You can say, "No, madam.

53:21.761 --> 53:24.223
I regret it is not possible,

53:24.346 --> 53:26.183
and I am so sorry."

53:26.306 --> 53:27.851
Then they buy it
not to look cheap.

53:29.269 --> 53:31.021
Every time.

53:31.147 --> 53:32.814
Is that still here?

53:34.857 --> 53:37.777
Yeah, so...

53:37.903 --> 53:40.906
maybe it will be in the sale.
Half price.

53:41.030 --> 53:43.408
Seventy-five guineas.

53:43.532 --> 53:45.995
Perhaps you have a rich uncle?

53:46.120 --> 53:48.329
I'm afraid not.

53:51.082 --> 53:53.210
<i>So, Mum, what about the collar?</i>

53:53.333 --> 53:54.878
Can we take that out too?

53:55.003 --> 53:57.672
Well, yes.
Yeah?

53:57.798 --> 54:00.842
And don't you think
a belt would be nice?

54:00.968 --> 54:04.470
Oh, yes, a belt might be nice.
Yeah. Just here.

54:04.596 --> 54:08.348
Um, but what about the frills?
I'm not too sure about...

54:08.475 --> 54:10.477
But you've always
loved the frills.

54:10.601 --> 54:13.229
Of course. Of course,
I like them. I was just...

54:13.354 --> 54:15.148
There's a lot of detail
in those frills.

54:15.274 --> 54:16.984
Yes, but they do make the dress

54:17.108 --> 54:19.236
a bit long, don't you think?

54:21.321 --> 54:25.200
It's just, it might be
a bit too long for me, yeah?

54:25.325 --> 54:27.910
We could maybe take it up a bit?

54:28.037 --> 54:29.578
Yeah.
Yeah?

54:29.705 --> 54:32.748
All right, we'll take it up.

54:32.875 --> 54:36.627
<i>♪ Just let me hear some of that
Rock and roll music ♪</i>

54:36.753 --> 54:38.587
Lisa!

54:42.092 --> 54:44.177
Oh, let me admire you.

54:44.303 --> 54:46.762
Oh, you look charming.

54:46.889 --> 54:48.139
Oh, thank you.

54:48.265 --> 54:50.266
Welcome, Lisa.
Have some punch.

54:50.390 --> 54:52.310
Thank you.
I made it myself.

54:52.435 --> 54:55.314
I think you try it
at least just once in your life.

54:55.438 --> 54:58.234
Be careful.
He put atom bomb in it.

54:58.359 --> 55:00.818
Oh, okay!
Come and meet Eva.

55:00.944 --> 55:04.322
Eva and Laszlo.
Nice to meet you.

55:04.447 --> 55:09.995
Their son, Miklos... Michael,
as he's insisting we call him.

55:10.119 --> 55:13.373
Hi.
He's proper Australian.

55:13.497 --> 55:16.210
And he's even forgetting
his Hungarian.

55:16.334 --> 55:17.752
Miklos, it's Lisa.

55:17.878 --> 55:19.755
Hi.
I hear you've done the Leaving.

55:19.879 --> 55:21.382
Yes.
Magda told me.

55:21.506 --> 55:23.384
She said you're a great
help at the store.

55:23.507 --> 55:25.760
She's my tower of strength.
She's clever.

55:25.885 --> 55:29.389
She's going on
to do great things.

55:29.514 --> 55:31.892
I've just done it too,
the Leaving.

55:32.016 --> 55:33.976
Oh, yes.
Are you going to university?

55:34.103 --> 55:37.064
I... I don't know.
Results in nine days.

55:37.188 --> 55:38.732
Yeah, nine days.
Yeah.

55:38.856 --> 55:42.777
<i>♪ Oh ♪</i>

55:42.903 --> 55:47.365
<i>♪ I, of course, replied... ♪</i>

55:47.490 --> 55:49.701
Fay.

55:49.825 --> 55:51.412
You look beautiful.

55:51.535 --> 55:53.289
Oh, thank you.
Sorry I'm a bit late.

55:53.413 --> 55:55.455
I had to catch
a train from the...

55:55.582 --> 55:56.791
You came to my home.

55:56.917 --> 55:59.253
No, it's really delightful.

55:59.377 --> 56:02.338
So who do you know?

56:02.463 --> 56:04.215
No one. No... Lisa.

56:04.341 --> 56:06.801
Lisa? Yes, I know Lisa.

56:06.927 --> 56:09.263
This is... This is Shandow.
Hello.

56:09.387 --> 56:12.974
And Maria and Bela.

56:13.100 --> 56:15.811
And there is Stefan.

56:15.935 --> 56:20.065
But he's deadly.
He's handing out atom bombs.

56:20.189 --> 56:22.276
Uh, here's Lisa.
Here's Lisa.

56:22.400 --> 56:24.444
Hi. Happy New Year.
How are you?

56:24.569 --> 56:27.781
So let her talk to Miklos,
who is called Michael.

56:27.906 --> 56:30.700
Uh, this is Rudi.
He's Hungarian.

56:30.826 --> 56:33.078
Rudi, this is Fay.

56:33.202 --> 56:35.580
She is Australian girl,

56:35.705 --> 56:38.374
not a refugee from Europe.

56:43.587 --> 56:46.506
Uh... Do you...?
Do you speak English?

56:46.633 --> 56:48.260
Of course.

56:48.385 --> 56:50.803
And Hungarian and German
and some French and Russian.

56:50.929 --> 56:52.388
A drink?

56:52.513 --> 56:59.021
<i>♪ Yet today my love
Has flown away ♪</i>

56:59.146 --> 57:00.896
<i>♪ I am without... ♪</i>

57:01.023 --> 57:04.901
Can you give me some punch?
I have some catching up to do.

57:05.026 --> 57:07.320
Matchmaking is a strain.

57:07.445 --> 57:09.905
So...

57:10.031 --> 57:15.704
The Serbs and the Croats
are not yet fighting,

57:15.828 --> 57:20.375
and Lisa is talking to Miklos...
Michael. Michael.

57:20.501 --> 57:23.170
Fay and Rudi are dancing.

57:23.294 --> 57:28.216
<i>♪ So I smile and say ♪</i>

57:28.342 --> 57:32.554
<i>♪ When a lovely flame dies ♪</i>

57:32.679 --> 57:37.892
<i>♪ Smoke gets in your eyes ♪</i>

57:43.523 --> 57:48.487
<i>♪ Smoke gets in your ♪</i>

57:48.611 --> 57:54.410
<i>♪ Eyes ♪</i>

57:58.914 --> 58:01.291
Oh, happy New Year!

58:10.384 --> 58:11.842
Okay.

58:49.381 --> 58:52.133
Thank you. Bye.
Bye.

59:05.730 --> 59:08.399
Lesley... Lisa,
we've been worried.

59:08.524 --> 59:10.611
Why, Mum?
Well, it's so late.

59:10.734 --> 59:13.447
Mum, it's New Year's Eve.
Yes, I know.

59:13.572 --> 59:16.032
But with people we don't know,
your dad's been anxious.

59:16.158 --> 59:18.576
Too right.
Who brought you home?

59:18.701 --> 59:20.662
Mr. Foldes.
Just him?

59:20.786 --> 59:23.122
No, Mum, his wife and son
were there too.

59:23.248 --> 59:25.542
"Foldes"? What kind of a name's that?

59:25.666 --> 59:27.293
They're Hungarians, Dad.

59:27.418 --> 59:28.795
Hungarian?

59:28.919 --> 59:31.880
God. Country's full
of bloody foreigners.

59:32.007 --> 59:34.676
But, Dad, we all came here
from Europe some time.

59:34.800 --> 59:37.304
Crikey, here we go. Now
I'm getting a history lesson?

59:37.428 --> 59:39.473
Now, you better
get to bed, Lisa.

59:39.598 --> 59:41.641
Get some sleep.
Yes, all right, Mum.

59:56.156 --> 59:58.907
I should have gone to mass this morning.

59:59.034 --> 01:00:00.118
Oh!

01:00:02.412 --> 01:00:04.206
Too late now.

01:00:05.456 --> 01:00:07.917
Well, there is a midday mass.

01:00:08.042 --> 01:00:09.377
Oh.

01:00:09.503 --> 01:00:11.463
I need some rest.

01:00:11.587 --> 01:00:15.008
I'm sure God will understand.

01:00:15.132 --> 01:00:18.386
Stefan?
Hmm?

01:00:18.511 --> 01:00:21.972
I think perhaps in one year,
maybe one year and a half,

01:00:22.097 --> 01:00:25.060
we must look for premises
in Woollahra.

01:00:25.185 --> 01:00:27.228
Even better, Double Bay.

01:00:28.647 --> 01:00:30.523
European fashions, huh?

01:00:30.649 --> 01:00:34.819
Exclusive fashions.
Expensive.

01:00:34.944 --> 01:00:37.321
Goode's doesn't know
the meaning of the word.

01:00:52.795 --> 01:00:54.715
Get out of the way.

01:00:59.553 --> 01:01:00.845
Good morning.
Sorry!

01:01:00.970 --> 01:01:01.971
That's quite all right.

01:01:12.814 --> 01:01:14.442
Thank you.

01:01:15.651 --> 01:01:16.903
Rudi?
Yeah.

01:01:17.027 --> 01:01:18.572
Are you looking for a frock?

01:01:18.696 --> 01:01:20.656
Of course.
What do you think?

01:01:20.782 --> 01:01:22.325
This one suits me?
Yeah.

01:01:22.451 --> 01:01:25.244
Rudi, you can't stay here.
Miss Cartwright will have a fit.

01:01:25.370 --> 01:01:30.250
In fact, my dear Lisa, I wish
to speak a few words to Fay.

01:01:30.374 --> 01:01:34.087
Oh, she's gone to morning tea.
Oh, wait, there she is.

01:01:39.259 --> 01:01:40.509
Hello.

01:01:42.929 --> 01:01:44.012
Hello.

01:01:46.099 --> 01:01:48.101
Fay,

01:01:48.225 --> 01:01:51.186
I've come to ask if you will
risk an outing with a reffo

01:01:51.313 --> 01:01:53.982
on a Friday night.

01:01:54.106 --> 01:01:55.733
Please think about it.

01:01:55.858 --> 01:01:59.278
Oh, I... I don't need
to think about it.

01:02:00.904 --> 01:02:02.531
Then I will call you
on Thursday night.

01:02:02.657 --> 01:02:04.199
Oh, no. No phone calls.

01:02:04.326 --> 01:02:06.201
My landlady,
she's a real old battle-ax.

01:02:06.328 --> 01:02:08.997
Don't worry, I will smother her
in Middle European charm.

01:02:09.121 --> 01:02:11.332
Soon, she will be looking
forward to my calls.

01:02:13.083 --> 01:02:14.127
Well...

01:02:15.377 --> 01:02:17.673
I should, um...

01:02:17.798 --> 01:02:20.841
Sorry. Oh, sorry.
It is fine.

01:02:44.323 --> 01:02:46.326
Thank you.
Ugh.

01:02:46.451 --> 01:02:50.204
It has been a madhouse here.

01:02:50.330 --> 01:02:54.167
Did I see that Hungarian wolf
prowling around?

01:02:54.291 --> 01:02:56.627
I think he's asked Fay out.

01:02:56.753 --> 01:02:59.047
Oh.

01:02:59.171 --> 01:03:00.965
Well, I have something to say,

01:03:01.090 --> 01:03:03.759
but I don't want
to translate it.

01:03:05.427 --> 01:03:07.429
It's still there.

01:03:07.556 --> 01:03:12.643
But I won't be responsible
if you rob a bank to buy it.

01:03:12.769 --> 01:03:15.605
And it could sell very soon
at 50 guineas.

01:03:15.731 --> 01:03:18.941
Oh, please don't say that.
Please don't sell it.

01:03:19.066 --> 01:03:22.821
Lisa, that is a promise
I could never make.

01:03:27.949 --> 01:03:29.994
<i>Fifty pounds for a dress?</i>

01:03:30.118 --> 01:03:31.996
I've never spent...
Guineas. Fifty guineas.

01:03:32.120 --> 01:03:33.956
That's 50 pounds, 50 shillings.

01:03:34.081 --> 01:03:37.042
Oh, well, that's even worse. You
could buy 10 dresses for that.

01:03:37.168 --> 01:03:39.336
Not like this one, Mum.
Oh, I don't know, Lesley.

01:03:39.462 --> 01:03:41.381
Lisa.

01:03:41.505 --> 01:03:44.174
I don't know what's happening
to you. I think it's that Magda.

01:03:44.300 --> 01:03:47.386
Now someone called Michael
has called you up. Who's he?

01:03:47.512 --> 01:03:49.096
Get the oven door, will you?

01:03:49.222 --> 01:03:51.724
He's a boy.
Just done the Leaving.

01:03:51.850 --> 01:03:54.518
His father drove me home on...
Yes, on New Year's Eve.

01:03:54.643 --> 01:03:56.979
But you said he was Hungarian.
He sounded Australian.

01:03:57.104 --> 01:03:58.856
Well, he was born here.

01:03:58.981 --> 01:04:01.192
Oh, well, in that case,
I suppose he is Australian.

01:04:01.317 --> 01:04:04.153
I suppose so.

01:04:04.278 --> 01:04:07.656
Get me a sherry, will you?
In the cabinet, in there.

01:04:10.118 --> 01:04:13.037
He put sheets in it,
your customers' sheets.

01:04:22.714 --> 01:04:24.548
Thief.

01:04:35.517 --> 01:04:38.562
Give me the pawn slip!

01:04:38.688 --> 01:04:40.147
That tickles!

01:04:49.865 --> 01:04:52.493
Here's what I think of
your pawn slip!

01:04:57.122 --> 01:04:58.541
Pig!

01:05:12.555 --> 01:05:14.098
So who was that director?

01:05:14.223 --> 01:05:16.058
René Clément.
Really charming.

01:05:16.184 --> 01:05:19.020
Oh, I'm sorry.
It's just, it's so sad.

01:05:19.144 --> 01:05:21.146
Oh, I've never seen
a French film before.

01:05:21.272 --> 01:05:24.024
Then I have arrived
just in time to rescue you.

01:05:25.359 --> 01:05:27.195
We shall see them all.

01:05:27.319 --> 01:05:29.155
<i>Les Enfants du Paradis.</i>

01:05:29.279 --> 01:05:30.782
<i>La Règle du Jeu.</i>

01:05:30.906 --> 01:05:33.200
<i>Pépé le Moko.</i>

01:05:33.326 --> 01:05:34.785
Are they all sad?

01:05:34.910 --> 01:05:37.664
No, not at all.
Some are most amusing.

01:05:37.788 --> 01:05:39.790
Oh!
Perhaps this wasn't a good choice.

01:05:39.916 --> 01:05:41.793
No, no, no, it was.

01:05:41.917 --> 01:05:43.378
I loved it.

01:05:46.881 --> 01:05:50.969
The Nazis conscripted me
when they occupied Hungary.

01:05:51.092 --> 01:05:54.472
They had me work
the railway lines.

01:05:54.597 --> 01:05:56.390
Not at all...

01:05:56.516 --> 01:05:58.266
pleasant.

01:05:59.894 --> 01:06:02.187
I escaped to Romania
and hid there

01:06:02.313 --> 01:06:03.772
for the remainder of the war.

01:06:03.899 --> 01:06:06.818
How is the goulash?
Oh, it's delicious.

01:06:06.943 --> 01:06:08.486
Go on.

01:06:11.030 --> 01:06:12.699
After the war,

01:06:12.824 --> 01:06:16.535
I was a bureaucrat in Budapest.

01:06:16.661 --> 01:06:19.162
Ha!

01:06:19.288 --> 01:06:22.458
What a line. I should...
I should write a song.

01:06:22.584 --> 01:06:27.422
But the Communists were
no improvement on the Nazis.

01:06:27.546 --> 01:06:30.592
So I escaped

01:06:30.717 --> 01:06:36.346
to the evil, capitalist West

01:06:36.472 --> 01:06:39.434
during the uprising in 1956.

01:06:39.558 --> 01:06:40.893
Oh, I've heard of that.

01:06:41.019 --> 01:06:44.397
First Italy,
then England and now

01:06:44.521 --> 01:06:47.609
Australia.

01:06:47.733 --> 01:06:50.945
This is where I intend
to make money. This is...

01:06:51.070 --> 01:06:53.196
the country of opportunity.

01:06:54.365 --> 01:06:57.077
Perhaps I am to be

01:06:57.200 --> 01:07:02.039
Australia's first Hungarian
ex-communist millionaire.

01:07:08.379 --> 01:07:09.672
Liszt.

01:07:14.718 --> 01:07:17.429
Tell me, do you prefer
Liszt to Mozart

01:07:17.555 --> 01:07:20.015
or Beethoven to both?

01:07:23.394 --> 01:07:25.562
I'm not sure.

01:07:25.688 --> 01:07:29.733
Well, I will take care of that.

01:07:29.859 --> 01:07:31.401
If you will permit me.

01:07:31.527 --> 01:07:35.196
You are very musical
or you could not dance so well.

01:07:37.700 --> 01:07:39.242
What about books?

01:07:39.368 --> 01:07:42.037
Oh, I've just finished
reading<i> Anna Karenina.</i>

01:07:42.162 --> 01:07:44.456
Ah.
Lisa lent it to me.

01:07:44.582 --> 01:07:48.753
Remarkable. I have found
an Australian intellectual.

01:07:48.877 --> 01:07:52.090
And Magda told me
they were all in England.

01:08:08.272 --> 01:08:10.525
Thank you.
Thank you, dear.

01:08:14.821 --> 01:08:16.237
Fay.

01:08:16.364 --> 01:08:17.699
Fay.

01:08:21.077 --> 01:08:22.828
Gee, look at that.

01:08:22.954 --> 01:08:25.330
Looks like he just wandered in
from the sheep show.

01:08:45.642 --> 01:08:47.186
Oh, Patty!

01:08:47.311 --> 01:08:49.146
Oh, goodness.
Oh, darling. Patty?

01:08:49.271 --> 01:08:51.439
Step away, everyone.

01:08:51.566 --> 01:08:54.026
No, please, move away.
We must attend to this lady.

01:08:54.152 --> 01:08:56.028
She's my wife.

01:08:56.154 --> 01:08:57.822
Oh, gracious.

01:08:57.946 --> 01:08:59.907
It's good you're here.
You can take her home.

01:09:00.033 --> 01:09:01.743
Patty, can you hear me?

01:09:01.867 --> 01:09:03.578
I have sal volatile.

01:09:03.703 --> 01:09:05.872
It never fails.
Here.

01:09:05.997 --> 01:09:08.124
- What happened?
- Is she okay?

01:09:09.374 --> 01:09:12.293
Just nice and slow.

01:09:12.420 --> 01:09:14.714
- Oh, go to hell.
- Now, now, now.

01:09:14.838 --> 01:09:17.758
Shh, shh. Now, you've had
a shock. Just be quiet.

01:09:17.884 --> 01:09:20.887
- Tell him to go to hell.
- Yeah, I've been to hell.

01:09:21.011 --> 01:09:24.305
I've just got back,
but I couldn't find my key.

01:09:24.432 --> 01:09:27.435
Gotta get my house keys off you.

01:09:27.559 --> 01:09:29.186
- Fainted?
- Mm-hmm.

01:09:29.311 --> 01:09:31.438
Right in the middle of
Ladies' Cocktail.

01:09:31.564 --> 01:09:34.192
Her husband was there too.
What was he doing there?

01:09:34.316 --> 01:09:35.734
I don't know.

01:09:35.860 --> 01:09:38.487
Well, all that sounds very odd to me.

01:09:38.612 --> 01:09:40.655
I wonder why
she hasn't had children.

01:09:40.782 --> 01:09:43.993
You should see her husband.
Now, Les... Lisa,

01:09:44.118 --> 01:09:45.744
what do you know about that?

01:09:45.869 --> 01:09:48.164
Well, he's completely gormless.

01:09:48.289 --> 01:09:49.916
Yes, well, so are lots of men,

01:09:50.040 --> 01:09:52.918
but it doesn't stop them
from becoming fathers.

01:09:55.337 --> 01:09:59.091
Well, I grew up in Bendigo,
in Victoria.

01:09:59.216 --> 01:10:04.430
But my dad died when I was 11,
in the war in New Guinea, so...

01:10:04.555 --> 01:10:07.475
We moved around
a lot after that.

01:10:07.599 --> 01:10:09.601
And I had to leave school
as soon as

01:10:09.727 --> 01:10:11.478
I turned 15 and find a job.

01:10:11.604 --> 01:10:13.147
So...

01:10:15.774 --> 01:10:19.360
You Australians
are a mysterious people.

01:10:19.487 --> 01:10:21.529
No one would guess that
this is a place

01:10:21.655 --> 01:10:24.700
that people can also suffer.

01:10:24.825 --> 01:10:28.036
It is the constant sunshine.

01:10:28.162 --> 01:10:31.166
It hides everything but itself.

01:10:34.042 --> 01:10:36.337
Where have you been?

01:10:36.462 --> 01:10:38.047
Wagga.

01:10:38.172 --> 01:10:39.632
Wagga?

01:10:39.756 --> 01:10:41.049
Wagga Wagga?

01:10:41.175 --> 01:10:43.219
Yeah, Wagga Wagga.

01:10:44.719 --> 01:10:47.140
Phil O'Connell, remember him?

01:10:48.474 --> 01:10:50.225
He was always asking me
to go down

01:10:50.351 --> 01:10:51.893
and give him a hand
with the pub.

01:10:52.020 --> 01:10:56.649
So I thought over Christmas,
New Year's.

01:10:56.773 --> 01:10:59.485
You didn't bother telling me,
of course.

01:10:59.609 --> 01:11:03.364
I am only your wife.
I wouldn't worry, would I?

01:11:03.489 --> 01:11:06.742
I wouldn't have to go tell lies
for you at Wonda's Tiles, or...

01:11:06.867 --> 01:11:10.745
Or turn up at Goode's
feeling sick and terrible.

01:11:10.872 --> 01:11:13.373
And why are
you back now, anyway?

01:11:13.499 --> 01:11:15.583
I suppose you ran out
of clean shirts.

01:11:15.709 --> 01:11:17.170
Well, I tell you what.

01:11:17.294 --> 01:11:20.422
You can clean your own
bloody shirts from now on.

01:11:36.231 --> 01:11:38.439
Sorry, I should've thought.

01:11:40.400 --> 01:11:42.528
Just had my mind
on other things.

01:11:42.652 --> 01:11:46.657
Yeah? Like what, for instance?

01:11:48.701 --> 01:11:50.661
Just felt...

01:11:50.785 --> 01:11:53.413
after that night,
you know, you...

01:11:53.539 --> 01:11:57.377
I just thought you wouldn't want
to see me again for a while.

01:11:57.501 --> 01:11:59.379
That night?

01:12:03.966 --> 01:12:06.467
With the nightdress?

01:12:06.594 --> 01:12:07.887
Yeah.

01:12:10.055 --> 01:12:11.766
Oh.

01:12:13.768 --> 01:12:15.269
Frank.

01:12:15.395 --> 01:12:16.979
Yeah?

01:12:18.940 --> 01:12:20.774
That's what's
been bothering you?

01:12:20.900 --> 01:12:23.111
Yeah.

01:12:23.236 --> 01:12:26.447
That's why you shot through?
Yep.

01:12:29.658 --> 01:12:30.659
Frank.

01:12:30.784 --> 01:12:32.369
Yeah?

01:12:35.372 --> 01:12:38.459
That was wonderful, that night.

01:12:42.297 --> 01:12:45.967
I just felt, you know, the way
I carried on was a bit...

01:12:46.091 --> 01:12:48.468
It was wonderful.

01:13:00.982 --> 01:13:02.900
You know what?
What?

01:13:03.024 --> 01:13:05.861
I am so hungry.

01:13:05.986 --> 01:13:07.528
Why don't you
go down the street,

01:13:07.654 --> 01:13:10.032
and get us some fish and chips?

01:13:10.157 --> 01:13:12.117
I'll call Mum when you're out.

01:13:12.243 --> 01:13:15.496
And don't be long.
I'm starving.

01:13:18.166 --> 01:13:20.667
Yeah, well...

01:13:20.793 --> 01:13:22.210
all right, then.

01:13:25.381 --> 01:13:27.549
So that was okay, that night?

01:13:27.675 --> 01:13:29.009
Oh.

01:13:29.134 --> 01:13:30.803
I'll say it was.

01:13:45.485 --> 01:13:47.986
We have heard nothing from Rudi.

01:13:48.112 --> 01:13:49.529
I wonder why.

01:13:49.654 --> 01:13:53.241
Well, he is obviously
engaged elsewhere.

01:13:53.368 --> 01:13:57.412
Correct. Did you see
who he left the party with?

01:13:57.538 --> 01:13:59.373
The prettiest girl?
Mm-hmm.

01:13:59.499 --> 01:14:01.916
The Australian girl, Fay.

01:14:02.043 --> 01:14:05.837
Well, Fay is an adult.
She can look after herself.

01:14:05.963 --> 01:14:08.548
She is a naive Australian girl,

01:14:08.674 --> 01:14:12.553
experienced, no doubt, only
with clumsy Australian men.

01:14:12.677 --> 01:14:15.013
And Rudi is a wolf
of a different color.

01:14:15.139 --> 01:14:16.973
This is melodrama.

01:14:17.099 --> 01:14:19.934
The reality probably is
that both are at a loose end.

01:14:20.061 --> 01:14:23.813
It suits them, for the time
being, to see each other.

01:14:25.483 --> 01:14:28.609
For a woman, it is never
only for amusement.

01:14:28.736 --> 01:14:30.738
The heart is engaged,
so it may be broken,

01:14:30.862 --> 01:14:32.197
and it will be my fault.

01:14:32.323 --> 01:14:34.866
Nonsense.
You merely introduced them.

01:14:34.992 --> 01:14:37.578
Now the ball
is in their court. Hmm?

01:14:37.702 --> 01:14:41.998
You're crossing the bridge
before the horse has bolted.

01:15:15.616 --> 01:15:18.869
Magnificent.

01:15:18.994 --> 01:15:22.622
Such a wonderful country.
And so empty!

01:15:25.126 --> 01:15:26.834
We must keep
its existence a secret,

01:15:26.960 --> 01:15:28.295
or all those bloody reffos

01:15:28.421 --> 01:15:30.047
will come racing down here
from Europe.

01:15:33.301 --> 01:15:37.472
Now for one of
your famous sandwiches.

01:15:47.982 --> 01:15:49.567
Mmm.

01:15:49.690 --> 01:15:50.859
Not bad.

01:15:52.944 --> 01:15:55.863
Are they different
from continental sandwiches?

01:15:55.990 --> 01:15:57.491
Yes,

01:15:57.617 --> 01:16:00.286
but the principle is the same.

01:16:01.662 --> 01:16:03.413
What are you thinking?

01:16:03.539 --> 01:16:06.666
Oh, nothing. Nothing much.

01:16:10.087 --> 01:16:11.964
Well, you remember
I was gonna tell you

01:16:12.088 --> 01:16:13.591
the rest of my story.

01:16:14.799 --> 01:16:17.219
Your disgraceful past.

01:16:17.344 --> 01:16:18.928
Yes.

01:16:19.053 --> 01:16:20.722
Am I going to be shocked?

01:16:22.599 --> 01:16:24.726
I think so.

01:16:24.851 --> 01:16:26.895
Well, proceed.

01:16:27.021 --> 01:16:28.564
Shock me.

01:16:32.359 --> 01:16:33.985
Well, when I was about 16,

01:16:34.111 --> 01:16:36.405
I had wanted to be
a ballet dancer.

01:16:36.530 --> 01:16:40.074
But my mother had had no money
for proper lessons,

01:16:41.285 --> 01:16:43.703
so, um, when I was about 18,

01:16:43.829 --> 01:16:46.665
I ended up in this...

01:16:46.790 --> 01:16:47.957
nightclub.

01:16:49.543 --> 01:16:52.004
Horrible.
Yes.

01:16:53.339 --> 01:16:56.174
And after a while, I, um...

01:16:56.300 --> 01:16:58.177
I met this man...

01:16:59.803 --> 01:17:04.599
Mr. Marlow,
and he was a businessman.

01:17:04.725 --> 01:17:07.143
He was older.

01:17:07.269 --> 01:17:09.729
At least 45.

01:17:11.815 --> 01:17:13.192
Mmm...

01:17:13.316 --> 01:17:15.861
He rented a flat for me

01:17:15.985 --> 01:17:17.821
in Kings Cross.

01:17:19.031 --> 01:17:20.949
And, um...

01:17:21.075 --> 01:17:23.952
And he would visit and...

01:17:32.252 --> 01:17:35.297
Were you in love
with this Mr. Marlow?

01:17:35.422 --> 01:17:37.090
Oh...

01:17:37.716 --> 01:17:39.759
No.

01:17:39.885 --> 01:17:45.391
And does this Mr. Marlow
still feature in your life?

01:17:51.730 --> 01:17:54.315
Believe me, Fay,

01:17:54.441 --> 01:17:56.736
the pope in Rome does not
have me high on the list

01:17:56.860 --> 01:17:59.697
being considered for sainthood.

01:18:05.953 --> 01:18:07.078
Magnificent!

01:18:11.541 --> 01:18:13.461
Six, seven, eight.

01:18:16.088 --> 01:18:17.590
And out, in, hip.

01:18:17.715 --> 01:18:21.218
Hip. Hip.
Step, cross shoulders.

01:18:21.342 --> 01:18:23.429
And around.

01:18:27.265 --> 01:18:29.435
And flick.

01:18:29.559 --> 01:18:32.020
Jump, kick, step, step.

01:18:32.145 --> 01:18:33.813
And one.

01:18:39.569 --> 01:18:42.238
Step, lunge, straight line.

01:18:46.577 --> 01:18:48.077
Diagonal.

01:18:48.203 --> 01:18:50.790
And around. First group.
<i>Hi.</i>

01:18:50.914 --> 01:18:52.666
Personalities.

01:18:55.502 --> 01:18:57.837
Big finish!

01:18:57.963 --> 01:18:59.256
And slow.

01:19:01.132 --> 01:19:03.343
- Thank you, ladies.
- Okay, girls.

01:19:03.469 --> 01:19:05.679
I suppose it could be worse.
Take a break.

01:19:05.805 --> 01:19:07.555
Another run-through at 6.

01:19:07.681 --> 01:19:09.932
So I think it was good
at the top this time. Okay.

01:19:10.058 --> 01:19:12.101
But the middle section needs
a bit of work.

01:19:12.226 --> 01:19:15.730
Want the old job back, Fay?
Not right now, Gerald.

01:19:15.856 --> 01:19:18.692
That was good, darling.
Thanks.

01:19:18.818 --> 01:19:21.277
So how are things
with your continental?

01:19:21.403 --> 01:19:23.405
Good.
What's his name?

01:19:23.530 --> 01:19:27.116
Rudi Janosi.
How do you spell it?

01:19:27.242 --> 01:19:32.164
Uh, J-A-N-O-S-I. What?

01:19:32.288 --> 01:19:34.290
Well, he could change it, you know.

01:19:34.416 --> 01:19:37.085
Quite a few of them do that.
Rudi won't.

01:19:37.210 --> 01:19:39.504
Rudi says the best thing
to do if there's anything

01:19:39.630 --> 01:19:44.425
unusual about you
is to brazen it out.

01:19:44.552 --> 01:19:45.927
You wanna be careful.

01:19:46.052 --> 01:19:47.261
You haven't known him long.

01:19:47.387 --> 01:19:48.888
I don't wanna see you get hurt.

01:19:50.973 --> 01:19:53.810
I'd rather be hurt by Rudi
than the types I used to know.

01:19:53.935 --> 01:19:55.478
Well, at least
with an Australian,

01:19:55.604 --> 01:19:57.146
you know where you are.

01:19:57.272 --> 01:19:59.940
Well, that's not too hot
if you don't wanna be there.

01:20:00.066 --> 01:20:03.738
At least with a continental,
you're going somewhere new.

01:20:03.862 --> 01:20:06.155
Yes. And it could be dangerous.

01:20:07.616 --> 01:20:09.701
Life is dangerous.

01:20:09.826 --> 01:20:12.621
You should hear
some of Rudi's stories.

01:20:12.746 --> 01:20:15.582
We live in a cocoon here.

01:20:15.707 --> 01:20:17.625
We don't know how lucky we are.

01:20:17.752 --> 01:20:20.545
I'll bet he knows
how lucky he is.

01:20:20.671 --> 01:20:22.505
He put the hard word on you yet?

01:20:22.631 --> 01:20:23.840
No.

01:20:29.637 --> 01:20:31.432
Do you love him, Fay?

01:20:32.974 --> 01:20:36.185
Yes. I reckon I do.

01:20:38.396 --> 01:20:39.607
Come here.

01:20:48.616 --> 01:20:50.951
I can't eat a thing.

01:21:05.257 --> 01:21:07.634
Lisa?
Hm?

01:21:07.759 --> 01:21:09.845
Have you found that one
in madam's size yet?

01:21:09.969 --> 01:21:11.971
Um, no, we don't seem
to have it.

01:21:23.191 --> 01:21:25.193
That girl's off with the pixies.

01:21:25.318 --> 01:21:28.113
Oh, well, you know why.
No, why?

01:21:28.237 --> 01:21:30.282
Well, her exam results
come out tomorrow.

01:21:30.407 --> 01:21:31.700
Well, tonight, if she goes

01:21:31.825 --> 01:21:34.076
down to the<i> Herald</i> building
at about 11.

01:21:36.663 --> 01:21:38.206
Here you are, madam.

01:22:00.938 --> 01:22:03.731
Hey, Ed, haven't you got a girl
that's just done the Leaving?

01:22:03.858 --> 01:22:05.942
Yeah.
They've finished setting the results.

01:22:06.067 --> 01:22:07.820
Get your tail down there.
Have a look,

01:22:07.944 --> 01:22:09.821
see how she went.
I'll look after this.

01:22:09.946 --> 01:22:13.533
Oh, no need to bother, mate.
Don't be a spoilsport.

01:22:13.659 --> 01:22:15.870
I'll go and check for you.
It's a big day for her.

01:22:15.994 --> 01:22:17.119
What school was it?

01:22:17.245 --> 01:22:19.832
She goes to North Sydney Girls.

01:22:19.956 --> 01:22:21.458
Ah.

01:22:21.582 --> 01:22:24.044
Let's go. We're ready for the next one.

01:22:29.884 --> 01:22:31.593
Sorry, excuse me.

01:22:31.718 --> 01:22:36.222
Sorry. Sorry. Sorry, excuse me.
I got four A's and a B!

01:22:48.109 --> 01:22:49.944
Is her name Lesley?

01:22:50.070 --> 01:22:51.905
Yeah.
Listen to this.

01:22:52.031 --> 01:22:55.449
Five A's and first-class honors
in English and history.

01:22:57.117 --> 01:22:58.745
That sounds all right,
doesn't it?

01:22:58.871 --> 01:23:00.789
Jeez, you're a cool one, Ed.

01:23:00.914 --> 01:23:02.833
It's bloody great,
that's what it is.

01:23:02.957 --> 01:23:04.793
Yeah?

01:23:04.918 --> 01:23:06.252
Huh.

01:23:07.379 --> 01:23:08.672
I should get back to work.

01:23:15.220 --> 01:23:18.514
I hear your daughter's
distinguished herself famously.

01:23:18.639 --> 01:23:20.558
Congratulations.
Wonderful news.

01:23:20.684 --> 01:23:22.269
Good on you, mate.

01:23:22.393 --> 01:23:25.021
Suppose she'll be off
to Sydney Uni?

01:23:25.145 --> 01:23:27.023
You must be very proud, Ed.

01:23:27.148 --> 01:23:28.483
Yeah, well...
Nice one, Ed.

01:23:28.608 --> 01:23:29.860
I'm not sure about that.

01:23:29.984 --> 01:23:31.695
I'm not sure about
the university.

01:23:31.820 --> 01:23:34.447
Surely, you wouldn't
waste brains like that.

01:23:34.572 --> 01:23:37.116
Tell her to come and see us
if she wants a cadetship.

01:23:37.242 --> 01:23:38.701
Congratulations, mate.

01:23:38.827 --> 01:23:41.121
But first,
university's the thing.

01:23:41.246 --> 01:23:44.750
Both my girls are there,
having the time of their life.

01:23:49.337 --> 01:23:51.422
What do you reckon, Ed?
Someone in the family

01:23:51.548 --> 01:23:53.634
got some brains, hey?

01:23:53.758 --> 01:23:55.342
Thanks, mate.

01:23:59.305 --> 01:24:01.849
Mum!
I know. Your father telephoned.

01:24:01.976 --> 01:24:04.184
Oh, gosh.
What did he say?

01:24:04.311 --> 01:24:06.271
Nothing much.
He was suffering from shock,

01:24:06.395 --> 01:24:08.106
or he wouldn't have phoned.

01:24:08.231 --> 01:24:11.192
Oh, Lesley... Lisa. This is
the proudest day of my life.

01:24:11.317 --> 01:24:12.944
Mine too, so far.

01:24:14.487 --> 01:24:17.448
♪ Volare ♪

01:24:17.574 --> 01:24:19.200
♪ Whoa ♪

01:24:21.327 --> 01:24:24.456
♪ Cantare ♪

01:24:24.580 --> 01:24:27.375
♪ Whoa ♪

01:24:42.432 --> 01:24:43.474
Frank.

01:24:45.352 --> 01:24:48.479
Yeah? What, would you like
some more toast?

01:24:50.273 --> 01:24:53.233
Put the bloody tray down
and come over here.

01:25:10.669 --> 01:25:14.339
Lisa, whose name is also Lesley.

01:25:14.463 --> 01:25:17.091
Oh, my young friend,
this is most happy day.

01:25:17.217 --> 01:25:19.094
Oh, thank you.
What's going on?

01:25:19.219 --> 01:25:20.553
Is she...?
Are you engaged?

01:25:20.678 --> 01:25:23.765
Oh, tush, at her age?
God forbid, no.

01:25:23.890 --> 01:25:26.935
She has obtained
most magnificent results

01:25:27.060 --> 01:25:28.186
in the Leaving Certificate.

01:25:28.311 --> 01:25:30.063
Good on you, Lisa.
Thank you.

01:25:30.189 --> 01:25:31.772
Congratulations.
Thank you so much.

01:25:31.899 --> 01:25:34.316
Stefan sends his love,
but we were not surprised.

01:25:34.442 --> 01:25:36.610
I read it in the<i> Herald</i>
this morning. Fabulous.

01:25:36.737 --> 01:25:37.611
Congratulations.

01:25:37.738 --> 01:25:38.947
Thank you so much,
Mr. Ryder.

01:25:39.073 --> 01:25:40.865
Lisa.

01:25:49.083 --> 01:25:51.584
Your results
were no surprise to me.

01:25:52.711 --> 01:25:54.295
Thank you.

01:25:54.420 --> 01:25:57.757
You're a clever girl.
I could see that.

01:25:57.883 --> 01:25:59.717
It's a pleasure
to work with you,

01:25:59.843 --> 01:26:01.469
and I'll be sorry
when you leave us.

01:26:01.594 --> 01:26:02.970
Oh.

01:26:03.097 --> 01:26:05.598
A clever girl

01:26:05.724 --> 01:26:11.020
is the most wonderful thing
in all creation, you know?

01:26:11.145 --> 01:26:12.898
You must never forget that.

01:26:13.022 --> 01:26:16.025
So you go to university

01:26:17.360 --> 01:26:20.321
and don't pass up
any opportunities.

01:26:20.446 --> 01:26:23.407
You just go away
and be as clever as you can.

01:26:23.533 --> 01:26:26.077
It's the best thing
you could possibly do.

01:26:26.203 --> 01:26:29.164
You and all the clever girls

01:26:29.288 --> 01:26:32.875
in this city and the world.

01:26:35.419 --> 01:26:38.339
Now, we'd better get on and sell
some cocktail frocks, hadn't we?

01:26:57.691 --> 01:26:59.027
<i>Where is he?</i>

01:26:59.153 --> 01:27:01.237
<i>He's out back.</i>

01:27:20.131 --> 01:27:22.675
Lesley, I can't see
what you want with these exams

01:27:22.801 --> 01:27:27.055
and first-class honors
and universities, being a girl.

01:27:27.180 --> 01:27:28.848
But still...

01:27:30.141 --> 01:27:32.185
congratulations.

01:27:32.311 --> 01:27:33.769
Done very well.

01:27:33.895 --> 01:27:36.355
Thanks, Dad.

01:27:38.817 --> 01:27:40.777
So, what do you want to do now?

01:27:42.029 --> 01:27:44.571
You know what I want to do.

01:27:44.698 --> 01:27:47.407
I'll think about it.
If you get that scholarship.

01:27:47.534 --> 01:27:50.410
Oh, she'll get it all right,
with that pass.

01:27:50.537 --> 01:27:52.246
Well, if you do,
I'll think about

01:27:52.371 --> 01:27:56.209
you going to university, okay?

01:27:56.335 --> 01:27:58.460
But if I do decide you can go,

01:27:58.586 --> 01:28:00.672
I don't want to hear you
mixed up with these

01:28:00.796 --> 01:28:03.217
libertarians and those
bloody communists,

01:28:03.341 --> 01:28:05.009
you'll be out
of this house in a shot.

01:28:05.135 --> 01:28:08.429
Understood?
Yes, Dad.

01:28:11.182 --> 01:28:12.642
Oh, um, I'll get it.

01:28:15.019 --> 01:28:17.021
What's this?

01:28:17.147 --> 01:28:19.858
Oh, that's salami.

01:28:19.982 --> 01:28:22.276
Lisa suggested it.

01:28:22.402 --> 01:28:24.611
All those bloody reffos
she's got to know.

01:28:30.076 --> 01:28:32.579
Huh. Salami?

01:28:32.703 --> 01:28:35.790
Reckon I could get used to that.

01:28:35.915 --> 01:28:37.334
We out of beer?

01:28:37.458 --> 01:28:40.254
No, Lisa suggested that too.

01:28:46.467 --> 01:28:47.593
Hmm.

01:28:48.762 --> 01:28:50.263
It was Michael Foldes.

01:28:50.389 --> 01:28:52.099
Who's he and what does he want?

01:28:52.223 --> 01:28:53.600
He passed the exams too.

01:28:53.725 --> 01:28:56.102
He wanted to know
if I'm doing anything tonight.

01:28:56.228 --> 01:28:58.437
Well, of course, you are.

01:28:58.563 --> 01:29:00.440
We're going out
to celebrate, aren't we?

01:29:00.565 --> 01:29:02.024
A slap-up meal in Chinatown.

01:29:02.150 --> 01:29:04.444
What do you reckon?
Oh, Dad.

01:29:04.568 --> 01:29:07.989
Crikey. Blokes ringing you up
and you finished school...

01:29:08.114 --> 01:29:09.823
It's hard to keep up with you,
Lesley.

01:29:09.950 --> 01:29:11.992
Lisa.

01:29:12.118 --> 01:29:14.620
Huh. God, what next?

01:29:16.164 --> 01:29:17.999
And have a look at you.

01:29:18.125 --> 01:29:20.001
Just have a look at you.

01:29:20.126 --> 01:29:22.254
Not bloody bad, eh?

01:29:22.379 --> 01:29:25.381
Heh, heh. Not bad.

01:29:36.142 --> 01:29:37.184
See you at the store.
Bye.

01:29:37.311 --> 01:29:38.477
Fay.

01:29:41.522 --> 01:29:43.565
Sorry.
That's all right.

01:29:46.027 --> 01:29:47.569
Where are we going?

01:29:47.695 --> 01:29:49.822
It's a surprise.

01:30:50.467 --> 01:30:53.927
I want your expert opinion.

01:31:12.406 --> 01:31:14.156
You see,

01:31:14.282 --> 01:31:17.368
there are harbor views.

01:31:22.580 --> 01:31:25.125
Maybe you will
dislocate your neck,

01:31:25.251 --> 01:31:27.836
but there are harbor views.

01:31:35.886 --> 01:31:38.138
What do you think?

01:31:38.264 --> 01:31:40.433
Well, I think it's...

01:31:40.558 --> 01:31:43.102
I think it's really lovely.

01:31:43.228 --> 01:31:46.856
But it's you
who has to like it. It's...

01:31:46.980 --> 01:31:49.734
I mean, it's your flat.

01:31:49.858 --> 01:31:52.446
What do you think?
Oh, you...

01:31:52.570 --> 01:31:54.738
misunderstand me.

01:31:56.866 --> 01:31:59.659
Uh.
Oh, you mean...

01:31:59.786 --> 01:32:02.497
You mean, it's for me.

01:32:02.620 --> 01:32:05.083
Just like Mr. Marlow, I...
Fay.

01:32:05.207 --> 01:32:06.709
I stay here and you...
Fay.

01:32:06.833 --> 01:32:09.420
You visit and...
Fay. Don't be bloody silly.

01:32:09.546 --> 01:32:11.630
Not like
Mr. Bloody Marlow.

01:32:11.755 --> 01:32:13.757
There, don't I sound like
a true Australian?

01:32:13.883 --> 01:32:16.634
No. No, this is for you.
No.

01:32:16.761 --> 01:32:19.179
For... me.

01:32:19.306 --> 01:32:22.516
For... us.

01:32:24.143 --> 01:32:26.480
I want you to marry me.

01:32:28.021 --> 01:32:29.524
Marry?

01:32:30.566 --> 01:32:32.485
Marry.

01:32:32.610 --> 01:32:34.613
Marry me?

01:32:35.862 --> 01:32:37.324
But...

01:32:37.448 --> 01:32:40.577
I mean, I don't...
I don't know anything about

01:32:40.700 --> 01:32:43.245
books, or music,
or art, or opera, or...

01:32:43.370 --> 01:32:44.913
No, bugger that.
Fay. Fay.

01:32:45.039 --> 01:32:47.583
Fay, you are honest.

01:32:47.708 --> 01:32:51.588
And sweet and beautiful,
and I am going

01:32:51.711 --> 01:32:53.797
to love teaching you all
about Bach and Mozart,

01:32:53.923 --> 01:32:56.176
and I love you.

01:32:58.178 --> 01:33:00.388
I adore you.

01:33:04.766 --> 01:33:06.645
Think about it
for as long as you like.

01:33:06.768 --> 01:33:08.770
I will give you
five minutes, at least.

01:33:08.896 --> 01:33:12.400
Shall I leave you alone...?
No, no, no. Don't leave me.

01:33:15.235 --> 01:33:17.030
The answer...

01:33:17.154 --> 01:33:18.948
is yes.

01:33:19.072 --> 01:33:21.493
Yes, I will.

01:33:31.294 --> 01:33:33.004
Yes.

01:33:37.090 --> 01:33:38.885
That was Rudi on the telephone.

01:33:39.009 --> 01:33:41.429
Oh?

01:33:41.554 --> 01:33:44.265
Yeah, he wants to borrow
50 pounds from me.

01:33:44.391 --> 01:33:45.933
Oh?

01:33:46.058 --> 01:33:48.269
Why?

01:33:48.394 --> 01:33:51.980
Oh, he wants to buy a
diamond ring or a sapphire.

01:33:52.106 --> 01:33:54.693
Oh, is he going into the jewelry business?

01:33:54.817 --> 01:33:57.362
Mmm, I don't think so.

01:33:57.486 --> 01:33:59.863
He wants to buy
an engagement ring for Fay.

01:33:59.989 --> 01:34:03.701
Sh...

01:34:03.826 --> 01:34:06.579
- Are you okay?
- Yeah.

01:34:06.704 --> 01:34:08.247
Uh...

01:34:08.373 --> 01:34:10.290
Engagement ring for Fay?

01:34:10.417 --> 01:34:12.085
Mm-hmm. Yeah.

01:34:12.209 --> 01:34:13.920
Stefan, uh,

01:34:14.044 --> 01:34:15.922
could you pour me a whiskey?

01:34:16.046 --> 01:34:17.507
All right.

01:34:20.884 --> 01:34:23.804
The whole thing is preposterous.

01:34:23.929 --> 01:34:26.432
How can they possibly
be happy together?

01:34:26.557 --> 01:34:30.895
They have absolutely nothing
in common. That's all.

01:34:31.019 --> 01:34:32.688
Well, having things
in common is not

01:34:32.813 --> 01:34:34.815
a condition
for a happy marriage.

01:34:34.940 --> 01:34:38.527
The point is that
they are happy together.

01:34:38.653 --> 01:34:41.030
Now, it's only the beginning.

01:34:41.154 --> 01:34:43.908
The middle and the end
must take care of themselves.

01:34:44.032 --> 01:34:46.494
At least he has not been
trifling with her.

01:34:46.619 --> 01:34:48.496
Not breaking her heart,
as I feared.

01:34:48.621 --> 01:34:52.292
But he could do, in future.

01:34:52.417 --> 01:34:55.962
I'm happy for them.
I wish them well.

01:34:56.086 --> 01:34:59.089
Still something of a shock.
Well, you know,

01:34:59.215 --> 01:35:01.049
one's friends can be shocking.

01:35:01.175 --> 01:35:04.219
It's one of
their appealing features.

01:35:35.669 --> 01:35:36.834
It is not here.

01:35:41.381 --> 01:35:45.636
Never let sentiment
interfere with business.

01:35:45.762 --> 01:35:50.098
To be sentimental about
business is to be weak,

01:35:50.223 --> 01:35:51.685
and to be weak is to fail.

01:35:51.809 --> 01:35:54.562
Do you understand?

01:35:54.687 --> 01:35:56.689
Yes, I suppose I do.
Yeah.

01:35:56.815 --> 01:36:00.777
So a man came to buy a present
for his daughter.

01:36:00.900 --> 01:36:03.321
And he liked the dress
very much.

01:36:03.445 --> 01:36:06.031
So...
So he got it for 50 guineas.

01:36:06.156 --> 01:36:10.910
I had given the dress
to Yvonne, to be wrapped,

01:36:11.036 --> 01:36:16.042
and he was telling me how happy
it would make his daughter.

01:36:16.166 --> 01:36:17.793
And...
And?

01:36:17.918 --> 01:36:22.173
And I told him that Yvonne
had made a big mistake,

01:36:22.297 --> 01:36:24.883
and that the dress
had already been sold.

01:36:27.470 --> 01:36:32.098
And never again will sentiment
interfere with business.

01:36:32.225 --> 01:36:33.560
I swear it.

01:36:37.396 --> 01:36:38.647
Here it is.

01:36:42.360 --> 01:36:45.655
Oh, but... But, Magda,
it's still 50 guineas.

01:36:45.779 --> 01:36:47.240
I don't have that much money.

01:36:48.825 --> 01:36:51.160
Miss Cartwright
came here yesterday,

01:36:51.284 --> 01:36:54.580
and she reduced all
the unsold dresses even further.

01:36:54.706 --> 01:36:56.999
To?
Thirty-five guineas.

01:36:58.208 --> 01:36:59.669
Oh, Magda.

01:36:59.793 --> 01:37:02.963
Thirty-five guineas?
I-I have the 10 from Dad,

01:37:03.088 --> 01:37:05.716
and my savings,
and the staff discount.

01:37:05.842 --> 01:37:07.342
I have 35 guineas.

01:37:12.556 --> 01:37:15.392
Perhaps you will be
a business woman.

01:37:15.518 --> 01:37:17.436
There are worse things.

01:37:21.607 --> 01:37:24.150
Everything seems to be
going smoothly, Miss Cartwright.

01:37:24.276 --> 01:37:25.819
True, Mr. Ryder.

01:37:25.944 --> 01:37:28.239
But we'll soon be losing half
of Ladies' Cocktail.

01:37:28.363 --> 01:37:29.948
Really?

01:37:30.073 --> 01:37:33.202
Well, young Lisa will be off
to Sydney University,

01:37:33.326 --> 01:37:35.621
but we always knew
she was only a temp.

01:37:35.747 --> 01:37:37.665
Correct,
but Mrs. Williams.

01:37:39.417 --> 01:37:41.460
Expecting.
Expecting?

01:37:41.586 --> 01:37:42.961
She doesn't look...

01:37:43.086 --> 01:37:45.172
How do you know?

01:37:45.297 --> 01:37:48.718
Years of observing ladies
in black, Mr. Ryder.

01:37:48.843 --> 01:37:51.011
- And Miss Baines.
- Yes?

01:37:51.137 --> 01:37:53.806
She's engaged. To a Hungarian.

01:37:53.932 --> 01:37:56.601
Hmm.
She's given us a month's notice.

01:37:56.725 --> 01:37:58.812
There's still Mrs. Symbo...

01:37:58.937 --> 01:38:02.148
Sym... Magda.

01:38:02.272 --> 01:38:05.275
That little boutique is worth
its weight in gold.

01:38:05.401 --> 01:38:08.111
Don't count on her being
around too long, Mr. Ryder.

01:38:08.236 --> 01:38:10.363
I've heard she's looking
for premises of her own.

01:38:10.489 --> 01:38:14.201
Oh, well, it's not
surprising, I suppose.

01:38:14.326 --> 01:38:17.995
Oh, well, they come and they go.

01:38:18.122 --> 01:38:21.584
At least, there's you and me,
Miss Cartwright.

01:38:21.709 --> 01:38:23.586
We're always here.

01:38:23.710 --> 01:38:25.630
We're the constant ones.

01:38:38.893 --> 01:38:41.353
Sorry, thank you.

01:38:41.479 --> 01:38:43.189
Please.

01:38:43.313 --> 01:38:45.023
Welcome! Come in.

01:38:45.149 --> 01:38:47.986
Thank you very much. Thank you.

01:38:48.110 --> 01:38:51.029
Wonderful. Come in, come in.

01:38:51.154 --> 01:38:53.991
So lovely, to finally meet you.

01:38:54.115 --> 01:38:55.576
Hi.
Welcome.

01:38:55.701 --> 01:38:58.412
Hi. You look lovely.
Thank you.

01:38:58.537 --> 01:39:04.042
Lisa. Oh, so beautiful.
It looks so wonderful on you.

01:39:04.167 --> 01:39:06.629
I suppose it does.
Too right, it does.

01:39:06.753 --> 01:39:08.714
Thank you.
So can we please sit?

01:39:08.840 --> 01:39:09.923
Yes, please.

01:39:10.090 --> 01:39:11.967
- Yes.
- Take a chair.

01:39:12.092 --> 01:39:14.845
Here, I'll take this.
Thank you.

01:39:16.389 --> 01:39:18.641
Thank you.
Excuse me.

01:39:18.765 --> 01:39:21.100
So I have arranged
a few delicacies.

01:39:21.227 --> 01:39:23.395
Well, I believe that
I arranged them.

01:39:23.521 --> 01:39:25.939
Well, you did. But I arranged
that you arranged them.

01:39:28.108 --> 01:39:29.944
Like always.

01:39:32.195 --> 01:39:35.114
Eva, can you do the honors?

01:39:35.241 --> 01:39:37.283
Can you do the honors?
Of course.

01:39:37.409 --> 01:39:39.244
Okay.

01:39:39.369 --> 01:39:41.163
Thank you.
Yes.

01:39:41.287 --> 01:39:42.915
I know what they are,
they're olives.

01:39:43.041 --> 01:39:44.541
Yes.

01:39:47.253 --> 01:39:48.963
Fay,

01:39:49.086 --> 01:39:51.088
I have to tell you something.

01:39:53.008 --> 01:39:57.846
We are going to be very rich
and have lots of children.

01:39:57.972 --> 01:40:00.683
At least four.

01:40:00.807 --> 01:40:03.143
Is that all right with you?

01:40:03.269 --> 01:40:05.062
Yes.

01:40:05.187 --> 01:40:07.022
Of course, it is.

01:40:13.029 --> 01:40:15.197
I have found the perfect place.

01:40:15.322 --> 01:40:16.698
Mmm.

01:40:16.824 --> 01:40:19.493
It's in the middle
of Double Bay.

01:40:19.618 --> 01:40:22.537
Magda's Fashions.

01:40:22.662 --> 01:40:24.122
What do you say?

01:40:24.247 --> 01:40:27.124
What can I say?
It is so appropriate.

01:40:27.251 --> 01:40:30.127
I want to show these
Australian ladies

01:40:30.254 --> 01:40:33.173
the meaning of the word
"style."

01:40:33.298 --> 01:40:35.175
And expense, I'm sure.

01:40:37.886 --> 01:40:41.098
Oh, forgive me, Mr. Miles,
for being inattentive.

01:40:41.223 --> 01:40:43.935
Oh, no worries, mate.
Oh, and it's Ed.

01:40:44.059 --> 01:40:47.270
Ed? Ed. Stefan.
Yeah, Ed.

01:40:47.395 --> 01:40:49.355
Stefan?
Mm-hmm.

01:40:49.482 --> 01:40:50.899
Stefan.

01:40:52.233 --> 01:40:54.070
This tastes great, what is it?

01:40:54.193 --> 01:40:56.530
Oh, duck liver pâté.
Sorry?

01:40:56.656 --> 01:40:57.657
Duck liver.

01:40:57.781 --> 01:40:58.991
Hm.

01:40:59.116 --> 01:41:00.827
Duck liver?

01:41:00.951 --> 01:41:02.160
Hmm.

01:41:02.285 --> 01:41:03.536
Strewth.

01:41:03.662 --> 01:41:05.288
Just won't think about it.

01:41:05.414 --> 01:41:07.667
If I can take everything else
that's been happening

01:41:07.791 --> 01:41:10.670
I guess I can take duck liver,
I suppose.

01:41:16.049 --> 01:41:18.344
No more lemonade, Lisa.

01:41:18.469 --> 01:41:22.264
In Hungary, you'd have
been drinking this for years.

01:41:22.389 --> 01:41:25.225
In moderation, of course.

01:41:38.906 --> 01:41:40.115
Now, cheers.

01:41:53.838 --> 01:41:55.464
So, um, engineering?

01:41:55.589 --> 01:41:57.466
Engineering, that's right.

01:41:57.590 --> 01:41:58.759
And you?

01:41:58.884 --> 01:42:01.428
Arts, of course, heh.

01:42:03.138 --> 01:42:05.599
Lisa,
when you finish university,

01:42:05.724 --> 01:42:07.560
what will you be?

01:42:12.313 --> 01:42:15.817
Um, I'm going to be
an actress, heh,

01:42:15.943 --> 01:42:18.862
or a poet or a novelist.

01:42:18.988 --> 01:42:22.074
Or maybe all three, heh.

01:42:22.200 --> 01:42:25.952
<i>♪ Don't lose
Your guiding light ♪</i>

01:42:26.079 --> 01:42:30.332
<i>♪ To the joys of the night ♪</i>

01:42:30.456 --> 01:42:32.125
<i>♪ Don't give up ♪</i>

01:42:32.250 --> 01:42:36.004
<i>♪ Even when your heart
Is breaking ♪</i>

01:42:38.381 --> 01:42:41.968
<i>♪ How strange
Everything feels ♪</i>

01:42:42.095 --> 01:42:46.056
<i>♪ My life's becoming real ♪</i>

01:42:46.181 --> 01:42:53.064
<i>♪ A work of art that's mine
For the making ♪</i>

01:42:53.189 --> 01:42:58.902
<i>♪ No matter what happens
Always be happy ♪</i>

01:42:59.028 --> 01:43:02.030
<i>♪ For a glorious day ♪</i>

01:43:02.155 --> 01:43:06.744
<i>♪ Our backs to the sun
The sparkling ocean ♪</i>

01:43:06.869 --> 01:43:11.497
<i>♪ It's a glorious day ♪</i>

01:43:17.797 --> 01:43:22.008
<i>♪ And I'm happy
Sitting here ♪</i>

01:43:22.135 --> 01:43:25.972
<i>♪ In the warm, salty air ♪</i>

01:43:26.096 --> 01:43:33.937
<i>♪ All the noises of the city
Disappearing ♪</i>

01:43:34.063 --> 01:43:37.941
<i>♪ And somehow, it's sublime ♪</i>

01:43:38.067 --> 01:43:42.029
<i>♪ To move through
Space and time ♪</i>

01:43:42.154 --> 01:43:49.661
<i>♪ In a boat
I now seem to be steering ♪</i>

01:43:49.787 --> 01:43:54.917
<i>♪ No matter what happens
Always be happy ♪</i>

01:43:55.042 --> 01:43:58.044
<i>♪ For a glorious day ♪</i>

01:43:58.171 --> 01:44:03.050
<i>♪ Our backs to the sun
The sparkling ocean ♪</i>

01:44:03.176 --> 01:44:10.098
<i>♪ It's a glorious day
And we go li, li, li-li, li ♪</i>

01:44:10.224 --> 01:44:17.022
<i>♪ Li-li, li, li
Li, li, li ♪</i>

01:44:17.148 --> 01:44:20.985
<i>♪ Always be happy ♪</i>

01:44:21.109 --> 01:44:25.030
<i>♪ For a glorious day ♪</i>

01:44:25.155 --> 01:44:29.617
<i>♪ It's a glorious day ♪</i>

